WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:08.933
- All right.

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:10.810
I see that attendee list growing

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.780
but we're gonna go ahead and get started

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.380
and the others continue to join.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.160
So thank you so much for joining us

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.340
for the Decolonization
of Development Economics,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.360
Early Voices from the
Global South discussion.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.730
This session opens a virtual symposium

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.160
devoted to race, ethnicity,
gender and economic justice

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.020
around the world.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.370
Hosted by Yale's Economic Growth Center

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.720
and Inclusion Economics Yale.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.640
I'm Catherine Cheney.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.840
I'm a reporter for Devex,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.870
focused on technology and
innovation in global development.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.110
I also moderate the Yale
Development Dialogues

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.750
which some of you may have seen

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.080
and I'm really thrilled to be
moderating this discussion.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.340
So we'll be discussing how a
pioneering group of thinkers

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.290
from low and middle income countries

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.520
informed the foundation

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.040
of the field of development economics.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.870
We're joined by panelists who will tell us

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.060
a little bit more about these economists

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.130
and the legacy that
they left on the field.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.420
Particularly when it
comes to the recognition

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.120
of this interplay between
race, ethnicity, gender,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.930
economic inequality
and global development.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.800
We'll have a conversation about how,

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.170
particularly in this time of
extreme and growing inequality,

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:16.680
these perspectives from history

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.713
can inform the future of
development economics.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.330
Before I introduce all of our panelists,

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.060
I'd like to introduce Rohini Pande,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.620
Henry J. Heinz the Second
Professor of Economics

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.440
and Director of the Economic
Growth Center at Yale

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.040
and Gerald D. Jaynes,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.270
A. Whitney Griswold Professor of Economics

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.740
and African American Studies
and Urban Studies at Yale.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.670
They're gonna provide a bit more context

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.000
on this conversation we're having today

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.470
and how it connects to
the larger symposium.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.840
So as I often say at the beginning

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.420
of these kinds of conversations,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.450
why this topic, why now?

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.993
We'll start with Rohini.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.780
- Thank you Catherine and
thank you everyone for joining.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.180
It's, I'm very happy to welcome all of you

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.480
on behalf of the Economic Growth Center

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.760
and it's the 60th anniversary

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:04.610
of the Economic Growth Center

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:09.472
and we're delighted to be
able to host this panel.

00:02:09.472 --> 00:02:11.320
So in the early 1950s,

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.670
as the Cold War began to gain steam

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.420
and colonization was ending,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.450
Ford Foundation was increasingly concerned

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.420
with the idea that
poverty causes conflict.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.380
They asked Lloyd Reynold who was then

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.790
the Economics department chair
at Yale to travel to Africa

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.090
to study the economic aspect
of this decolonization.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.200
His daughter called this trip

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.810
a turning point in her father's life

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.660
because it inspired him to
change the focus of his work

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.260
from labor to development economics.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.610
Interestingly his first
development economics research

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:45.670
was on wages and productivity
in Puerto Rican factories.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.970
And he interviewed over
1000 workers in 85 factories

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.830
and the most striking
fact of the paper he wrote

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.290
was to note to say that
worker and foreign behavior

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.400
was remarkably similar to what he had seen

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.300
in more industrialized nations

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.320
and he directly challenged myths

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.123
that low income countries
would look very different.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.020
After he came back he went to
the Ford Foundation for a year

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.050
where he met Simon Kuznets.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:14.100
Simon Kuznets was at that
point at Johns Hopkins.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.250
He was later a Nobel Laureate

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.300
and he often talked about the idea

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.090
that we needed a Center that would focus

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.010
on the comparative study
of national economies.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.760
At that time there was
very little information

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.270
about these newly decolonizing economies

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:31.410
and so Kuznets and Reynolds together

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.130
ran a seminar at the National
Bureau of Economic Research

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.650
in 1958 which then became
the basis of a proposal

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.433
to the Ford Foundation for
Economic Growth Center.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.450
So in December 1960,
the UN General Assembly

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.290
famously passed the 1514 UN Resolution

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.320
which was a Declaration
Granting Independence

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.360
of Colonial Countries to People
and very soon after that,

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.270
in 1961, the EconOmic
Growth Center was founded

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.450
with a 10-year Foundation grant.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:02.340
It then went on to become
an endowed center later on

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.690
but when it started,

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.470
the largest organized program it had

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.560
was field study of recent economic growth

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.873
in selected countries.

00:04:10.874 --> 00:04:14.200
The idea that Lloyd Reynolds
as the Chair put forward

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.470
was that he would hire
Assistant Professors

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.050
and in one of the earlier reports,

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.929
he described the production site.

00:04:19.929 --> 00:04:23.940
Each of these men would
spend six to nine months

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.390
in New Haven learning language
skills and analysis skills.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.570
They would spend up to two
years in the country chosen

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.490
and then on return they
would combine teaching

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.920
and working material.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.710
It was certainly an
extremely important way

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.260
for the Economics department to grow.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.350
25 assistant professors at Yale

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:41.810
were hired under this program.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:45.230
And many of them went on to be,

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.080
to be some of the first generation

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.627
of development economists
in the U.S. universities.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.080
Today when I look at the history

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.920
of those early years of EGC

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.640
which are very well documented

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.940
through the Ford Foundation Archives,

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.340
you know some aspects have
certainly not dated well.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.920
I think there were a
lot of young, white men

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.230
who were being sent to
the colonies to study them

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.270
but I think in other ways,

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.080
it did identify a set of issues

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.970
that perhaps development
economics lost a bit of focus on

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.980
along the way when it
became very technocratic

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.280
which were issues of
thinking of inequality,

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.374
asking questions of what exactly

00:05:20.374 --> 00:05:24.420
do people in these countries,
both scholars, policymakers

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.740
and others imagine as the focus.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.090
And then I think one reason
why I'm really excited

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:32.950
to be doing this conference with Gerry

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:35.250
is that the early focus was very much

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.230
that it's a continuum of issues

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:40.510
that range from the U.S.
to lower income countries

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.090
when we think of issues of inequality,

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.190
when we think of issues of how do we have

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.650
process of economic
growth to work for all.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:49.370
So I hope very much that this panel

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.170
can set the stage by reminding us again

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.030
of what were the discussions
in those early days?

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.230
And with that, let me
hand it over to Gerry.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:05:59.257
- Thanks Rohini.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:06.570
- Well thank you Rohini and
welcome also to everyone.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.330
What I'd like to do is
just take a few minutes

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.330
to talk about what it is at
least that I think of as,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.620
when I think of the phrase
decolonization of development.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.220
And by that I simply
mean the right of nations

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.190
to self determination.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:30.030
Now a right that's of course
only partly won at this point

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.910
but if we consider it and what it means

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:37.480
for those peoples who are involved,

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.330
I think it floats throughout

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:43.740
as an underlying thesis
of the entire symposium

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.013
that we'll be doing.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.761
So to begin with, when the
Growth Center opened during 1961,

00:06:50.761 --> 00:06:54.240
let's keep in mind over
80% of the earth's people

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.010
lived on less than a dollar a day.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:01.060
Women especially were
engulfed in exploitation,

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.900
whether they were in a
wealthy or poor country

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:09.210
and gross illiteracy reigned globally.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:14.210
The Cold War dominated
economic policymaking worldwide

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.130
and colonial powers who were
heirs to a world economy

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:22.550
born during a global slave trade

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.840
were reluctantly relinquishing,

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.680
at least their formal
control over people of color

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.470
across the globe.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:32.300
Beneficiaries of the exploitation

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.240
were living in deep denial.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.140
My formal introduction
to all this occurred

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:42.270
rather coincidentally
at the exact same time

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.500
that the Growth Center was opening up.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.430
When Jean Soderstrom,

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.320
the white daughter of a Republican family

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:54.120
told my otherwise all white
ninth grade social studies class

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.240
that we were about to study for the spring

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.330
contemporary independence movements.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:04.090
So I began to learn a lot more about names

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:07.850
like Gandhi and Nehru, Fidel Castro,

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:12.540
Che Guevara, Patrice
Lumumba and Kwame Nkrumah.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.450
All leaders balancing tightropes

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.620
between Cold War powers who were vying

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.250
to control developing countries'
resources and consumers.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:26.680
Now suspicious of capitalist colonizers,

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.260
the newly formed nations
tried alternative paths

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.520
of economic development.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.940
The marginalized economist
must recognize that

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:38.661
the demand for self determination

00:08:38.661 --> 00:08:42.623
without abandoning rigorous thinking.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.000
She needs methodologies and models

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.240
that elucidate the problems of the world

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:53.530
but also speak to her
marginalized identity

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.510
in a profession that has unfortunately

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:58.700
been steeped in ways of thinking

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.670
that often underpin her marginalization.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:07.280
Most adopt a premise of
post-colonial studies.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:11.070
Colonial rulers are unreliable narrators

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.940
of their subjects' conditions.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:18.294
Two economists whose careers
exemplified this tension,

00:09:18.294 --> 00:09:22.230
who I met along my way in
the early days at Yale,

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:27.220
at the Economic Growth Center
were Carlos Diaz Alejandro,

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.080
an EGC colleague of eight years

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.550
and Sir W. Arthur Lewis
with whom I co-taught

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:38.300
during a year he visited
the Economic Growth Center.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:41.190
I think it was 1979.

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:44.910
Born respectively in Cuba and Saint Lucia,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:47.040
their Caribbean origins,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.420
where slave trading and its aftermath

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.150
blended people of Asia, Africa, Europe

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:55.840
and New World Natives,

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:00.840
creating colonized peoples
who shared my status

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:06.260
as a member of the world's
marginalized population.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:10.210
Carlos, the Cuban refugee who loved Cuba

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:14.010
for what it might become and not its past

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:15.860
shattered stereotypes

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:19.700
of a Cuban immigrant blindly
opposed to socialism,

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:21.840
real or imagined.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.740
He supported renewing U.S. Cuban relations

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.920
even during the Cold War.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:31.560
He opposed colonialist power relations

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:35.650
throughout Latin America and his refusal

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.670
to back U.S. imperialists' intrusion

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:42.490
into Nicaragua during the 1980s,

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:46.070
are a stark revelation of the open tension

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.090
that exists in the perspective

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:51.440
of a well-trained economist familiar

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:55.820
with both the growth potential of markets

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:58.630
and the human rights of peoples determined

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.680
to find their own voices.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:05.374
W. Arthur Lewis, his celebrated paper,

00:11:05.374 --> 00:11:09.900
Economic Development with
Unlimited Supplies of Labor

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.490
for which he won a Nobel Prize,

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:15.420
becoming the first and only person

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:19.410
of African descent to
do so also exemplifies

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:22.003
what I am calling this postcolonial turn.

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.520
His ideas of surplus labor,
disguised unemployment,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.480
postulation of zero marginal product,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.720
leading to contemporary models

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:35.480
of family utility maximization

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:40.480
and intra-family equity were all anathema

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:42.110
to the neoclassical model

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.663
his contemporaries were formulating.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:50.550
Isolate the essence of his
British-style economic training

00:11:50.905 --> 00:11:55.905
which attempts to reconcile
his rational modeling

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.850
with his gut-wrenching life experiences

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:01.940
in the Caribbean and elsewhere

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:06.940
and we see the beginnings of a atmosphere

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:14.680
about which his ideas
might have been formed.

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:17.990
If we throw in his experience in Britain

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:22.820
where he was engaged in
near daily ferocious debates

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.910
over politics and economic development

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.000
with the likes of CLR James of Jamaica

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.165
and Eric Williams of Trinidad Tobago

00:12:34.165 --> 00:12:38.180
who along with other Caribbean thinkers

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.780
like Frantz Fanon of Martinique

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:43.960
and Aime Cesaire, also of Martinique

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.890
whose discourse on colonialism

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:51.220
and his notebook of a
return to my native land

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.397
along with W. E. B. Du Bois',

00:12:53.397 --> 00:12:55.990
"Strivings of the Negro People"

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:00.990
might arguably be called
the foundations of the field

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.240
of postcolonial studies.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:06.290
When we see all this and we
think about that training

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:09.430
and that experiential
existence as a member of

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.540
marginalized people of the world,

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.720
we can think that this is the surroundings

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:18.330
that began Lewis' framework
for discussing the reality

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.430
of economic development and policymaking

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:25.333
in a multi-sector complexity.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:30.010
- Thank you Jerry and
you're really setting us up

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:32.320
for success here in terms
of something I had asked

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:35.760
all of our panelists to think
about which is you know,

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.220
our audience members might
have different levels

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.050
of familiarity with some of the economists

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:41.320
who are gonna come up

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.350
and I asked each of our panelists

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:46.530
to really tell us about
these people as people.

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:47.978
Who were they?

00:13:47.978 --> 00:13:50.500
What impact did their ideas have?

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:51.860
What challenges did they face?

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.400
What legacy have they left?

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:57.100
And that's a great preview of Arthur Lewis

00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:59.250
and we're gonna hear a little
bit more in just a moment.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:01.510
So with that, I wanna
go ahead and introduce

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.030
three additional panelists.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:06.950
Ravi Kanbur, David
Engerman and Amna Qayyum.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:09.300
Ravi Kanbur is a Professor of Economics

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:10.880
at Cornell University.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:13.620
He researches and teaches
in development economics,

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:16.170
public economics and economic theory

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:17.970
and some of his research and writing

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.330
has explored Arthur Lewis
who you'll hear about

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:22.090
a bit more in a moment.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:22.923
David Engerman

00:14:22.923 --> 00:14:25.990
is Leitner International
Interdisciplinary Professor

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:27.950
in the Department of History at Yale

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:30.040
and the Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:31.470
at Yale University.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:34.640
And Amna Qayyum is a postdoctoral fellow

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:38.210
at the Jackson Institute for
Global Affairs, also at Yale.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:39.680
Each of them have insights on

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:42.380
how some of these early founders
of development economics,

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:43.920
Arthur Lewis and others,

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.200
shaped our understanding of development.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:47.550
And I wanna note for our audience

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.930
that I certainly have a lot
of questions for our panelists

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.700
but we want to make this as
useful for you as possible.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.260
So please go ahead and
use the Q and A function

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:58.130
on the Zoom webinar to ask
your questions throughout

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:00.500
and I'll ask you to keep
them coming throughout,

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.800
not wait until the end so we can make this

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.010
more of a conversation and
get your perspectives in

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:06.470
from the get go.

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.663
Ravi, I'd love to start with you.

00:15:08.663 --> 00:15:11.600
So as I mentioned, this was a preview

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:13.910
that Gerry provided on Arthur Lewis.

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:16.250
I wonder if you can tell
us a little bit more

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:17.540
about his work,

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:20.270
the challenges he faced including racism

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.410
as you've written about and the legacy

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.380
that he's left on the field.

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:26.660
- Well thank you, thank you
very much indeed Catherine

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:31.300
and my thanks to Rohini and
to Gerry for the invitation.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:35.930
I'll pick up from where
Gerry started on Arthur Lewis

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:38.480
just to say that Arthur
Lewis was born in 1915

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:43.480
in the West Indies and he died in 1991

00:15:44.577 --> 00:15:47.400
and then having won the
Nobel Prize in 1979.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:51.427
So that sort of locates him in time.

00:15:51.427 --> 00:15:54.210
And as Arthur, he was an
absolutely brilliant student.

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.900
As Gerry said, he's an
absolutely brilliant student.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:59.360
Won a scholarship to the
London School of Economics

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:04.360
and in the 1930s, he interacted
with this group of radical,

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.260
young Black radical students.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:08.270
A lot of them from the West Indies.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:10.300
By the way including also Paul Robeson

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.170
who at the time was at Oxford,

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:16.820
not at the LSE and you
know, it was that milieu,

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:20.358
and it led to quite a
radical stance on his part

00:16:20.358 --> 00:16:23.240
in the 1930s and 1940s.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:24.690
I think what's interesting to explore

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:28.802
is how Arthur Lewis over the years

00:16:28.802 --> 00:16:31.430
became more mainstream so to speak

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:33.720
and moved a little bit more to the center

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:35.125
through his engagement

00:16:35.125 --> 00:16:38.783
with actually the decolonization
process and so on.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:44.680
And he faced quite
considerable racism in Britain.

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:50.040
One particular thing, you
know he was appointed to the,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.010
equivalent position of
Assistant Professor at the LSE.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.590
The first Black man in
history to have done so

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:58.400
but at the time of the appointment,

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.050
the Chair of the Department felt,

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:01.910
although the Chair,

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:04.620
the perfect right to make the
appointment felt he had to

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:06.700
write to the Board of Governors,

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:09.467
the trustees in other words
of the LSE because he said,

00:17:09.467 --> 00:17:13.016
"We're going to be appointing
a Black man to this position."

00:17:13.016 --> 00:17:13.849
And this is at the LSE,

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:14.850
the London School of Economics

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:17.190
which was founded by the
great Fabian socialists,

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:18.750
Sidney and Beatrice Webb.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.720
So if it's in that institution
you have this going on,

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.040
then you can imagine what
was going on institutionally

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.670
and in the rest of society.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:30.150
So that's just one example
of these things that he faced

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:34.640
and he was quite radical at
that time in his writings

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:36.080
and so on.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.570
Now quite interestingly,

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:40.943
he had a big interaction with Ghana.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:44.250
And Gerry mentioned Kwame Nkrumah.

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:47.020
Kwame Nkrumah was again, a
very radical leader of Ghana

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:48.880
who led Ghana to independence

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.040
and Ghana became independent in 1957.

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:55.220
And in March, and in October 1957,

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.210
Arthur Lewis arrived in Ghana

00:17:57.210 --> 00:18:00.531
as the Chief Economic Advisor
to the new government.

00:18:00.531 --> 00:18:03.740
And indeed the reason why
he was invited to do so,

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:06.070
because the radical government

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:08.020
of the so-called Convention People's Party

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:10.270
which won the election,
which swept the election,

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:14.073
thought that he would be bringing
very radical perspectives.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:16.900
Support for industrialization,

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.570
support for state this and
that, et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.900
And in fact, it didn't
quite turn out that way.

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:26.920
Because although Arthur Lewis
was no market fundamentalist,

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.370
he was no whatever.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:30.920
But he actually looked at
the situation on the ground

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:32.930
and he argued, actually
in an earlier report

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:35.590
which he had written that
in fact Ghana should focus

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:37.480
more on the agricultural sector,

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:40.610
not on industrialization.

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:42.710
Whereas what Nkrumah was pushing for

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:45.173
was a rapid industrialization,

00:18:45.173 --> 00:18:48.820
big infrastructure projects
and so on and so forth.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:50.290
And that led to a tremendous conflict

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:52.810
between him and Arthur Lewis

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:55.820
and actually the interaction
between economics and politics

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:58.123
is also laid out very
clearly in this thing.

00:18:58.968 --> 00:19:00.130
In one of the letters

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:02.500
that Kwame Nkrumah wrote to Arthur Lewis,

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:07.450
after Arthur had criticized
him for the economic plan.

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:09.077
He said,

00:19:09.077 --> 00:19:11.887
"The advice you have given
me, sound though it may be

00:19:11.887 --> 00:19:14.707
"is essentially from the
economic point of view

00:19:14.707 --> 00:19:16.657
"and I have told you on many occasions,

00:19:16.657 --> 00:19:18.677
"I cannot always follow this advice.

00:19:18.677 --> 00:19:22.590
"As I am a politician, I
must gamble on the future."

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.681
Nkrumah very famously said had said,

00:19:24.681 --> 00:19:27.720
"Seek ye the political kingdom first."

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:32.490
And this tension between
what Arthur Lewis considered

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:34.910
to be rational economic advice

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:36.450
and the political realities on the ground

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:39.217
is a feature of that interaction between,

00:19:39.217 --> 00:19:43.723
of Arthur Lewis' work in Ghana.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:47.120
And he then went on
actually later on, sorry.

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:49.077
He went on later on actually
to write a book called,

00:19:49.077 --> 00:19:51.110
"Politics in West Africa"

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:53.670
in which he talked about
this, this conflict.

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:55.570
Actually in my writings I've sort of,

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:56.880
let me just conclude with this Catherine.

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:00.970
In my writings I have sort
of argued that perhaps,

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:02.743
that perhaps Nkrumah had a point.

00:20:03.969 --> 00:20:06.280
That in fact,

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:10.450
Nkrumah had to balance
tremendous cleavages in Ghana

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:11.770
across different ethnic groups,

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.750
across tribes and so on and so forth.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:18.389
So what may have seemed
economically irrational

00:20:18.389 --> 00:20:21.250
may have been the way that he kept

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:22.620
the whole show on the road.

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:26.230
By balancing the different
interests and so on.

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:28.490
And this tension between economic

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.070
and political realities in a society

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:32.730
with deep cleavages

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:37.730
and seemingly economically
rational policy prescriptions

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:41.070
is something, is a theme that I think

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:43.170
we should be focusing on

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:46.363
as we think about
economic policy analysis.

00:20:47.836 --> 00:20:48.742
- Absolutely.
- So let me

00:20:48.742 --> 00:20:49.882
stop there Catherine and I'll come back

00:20:49.882 --> 00:20:51.143
with whatever later on.

00:20:52.177 --> 00:20:53.510
- Thank you, no I had a
followup question for you

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:55.463
which perhaps I'll save
so that we can bring in

00:20:55.463 --> 00:20:57.060
some other perspectives from history

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:58.860
but I wanna come back to you on it.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:00.210
I just thought it was really interesting

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.450
that you mentioned how Arthur Lewis

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.800
moved from the radical to the mainstream

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:06.350
and I wanted to better understand

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:07.510
the evolution of his thinking

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:09.040
but maybe we can return to that.

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:12.030
I also really appreciate
that point you concluded on.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:14.560
This tension between political realities

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:18.230
and what may seem like
economically rational

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:19.230
policy prescriptions.

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:20.950
So I hope in terms of finding

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:22.060
a narrative thread between

00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:23.470
these different figures from history

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:26.330
we can understand how
economic theory bumped up

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:30.000
against reality and how
that shaped this evolution

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.429
of their thinking.

00:21:31.429 --> 00:21:32.759
But thank you Ravi, that's really helpful

00:21:32.759 --> 00:21:34.630
and we'll come back to
you with some of that.

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:37.360
So David, I know you've done research on

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:39.888
a number of development economists

00:21:39.888 --> 00:21:43.760
who were at Cambridge
University around the same time

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:44.650
and their legacy.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:47.450
So can you tell us a little bit more?

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:49.240
Maybe zoom in on a few examples

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:51.850
in terms of who were these
people and what legacy

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:53.250
have they left on the field?

00:21:54.374 --> 00:21:55.410
- That's right, thanks Catherine

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.180
and thanks to our organizers.

00:21:57.180 --> 00:21:59.270
I'm writing a history
of development economics

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:02.900
as told through the lives and
works of six practitioners.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:05.719
All six were born in the early 1930s

00:22:05.719 --> 00:22:08.220
in Britain's South Asian colonies.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:09.880
They were too old to be Salman Rushdie's

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:14.353
Midnight's Children born
of independence in 47, 48.

00:22:14.353 --> 00:22:16.300
We might call them instead
Midnight's Teenagers.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:20.750
All six, as you say
Catherine came to Cambridge.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.040
At that point, certainly thought of itself

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:24.540
as the premiere department in the world

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:26.960
and had a pretty good case for doing so.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:29.640
All six went on to greatness at national,

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.710
regional and global scales.

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:33.610
One of them, Amartya Sen

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.580
won a Nobel Prize in economics.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.200
Another, Manmohan Singh,

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:40.770
served a decade as
Prime Minister of India.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:45.234
A third, Jagdish Bhagwati is
a perennial Nobel contender.

00:22:45.234 --> 00:22:49.180
A fourth, Mahbub ul Haq
invented the now ubiquitous,

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:50.370
Human Development Index,

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:51.990
actually with some help from Amartya Sen

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:55.040
and others who I'll
talk about in a second.

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:57.940
And two others shaped policy
and public life in Sri Lanka.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:23:00.870
That's Lal Jayawardena and in Bangladesh,

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.070
that's Rehman Sobhan,
who actually worked as a,

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:04.538
essentially a freedom fighter

00:23:04.538 --> 00:23:09.538
for the independence
of Bangladesh in 1971.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:11.570
All six of them at one point or another

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:14.850
worked at this exact nexus
of policy and economics

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:17.459
that Ravi just raised and we can come back

00:23:17.459 --> 00:23:19.690
to that issue as well.

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:23.132
All of them also faced this
important dilemma which is they,

00:23:23.132 --> 00:23:25.910
at Cambridge they learned economics

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:28.830
that was based almost entirely
on the economic experience

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:30.600
of the Atlantic world.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.239
But they became interested
in economics initially

00:23:33.239 --> 00:23:37.250
in order to understand the
world in which they were raised.

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:40.920
What was then the third
world, now the Global South.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.867
Mahbub ul Haq has always
had a ready answer

00:23:43.867 --> 00:23:46.490
for how to handle this dilemma.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:49.150
Even in his very first week at Cambridge.

00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:51.680
He told Amartya Sen as the
two of them were reflecting

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:53.870
on their very first undergraduate lecture

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:55.900
by the legendary Joan Robinson,

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:57.582
Haq said that Cambridge economists

00:23:57.582 --> 00:24:00.270
were asking all the wrong questions.

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:01.740
But instead of ignoring them,

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:04.241
Haq told Amartya Sen

00:24:04.241 --> 00:24:07.047
we must learn the tools
of modern economics.

00:24:07.047 --> 00:24:08.347
"Only then" he said,

00:24:08.347 --> 00:24:10.137
"would we be taken seriously.

00:24:10.137 --> 00:24:11.367
"And besides," he continued,

00:24:11.367 --> 00:24:12.497
"we can use those tools

00:24:12.497 --> 00:24:15.310
"to pose and answer the right question."

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:17.770
And certainly by the record
they did ask the right questions

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:20.610
about the role of the state
and markets in development,

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:23.430
about how to build a
newly independent nation.

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:26.030
About the relationship
between poverty and inequality

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:26.863
and that actually about

00:24:26.863 --> 00:24:28.530
the ultimate purposes of development.

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:30.723
What is development for?

00:24:30.723 --> 00:24:32.810
In the work I'm doing
I'm trying to write it

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:36.010
as an embedded history
of development economics.

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:37.660
It's embedded in time and place.

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:39.650
Specifically in the Global South

00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:41.502
and this kind of cuts against the idea

00:24:41.502 --> 00:24:43.920
raised by many historians
who see development

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:45.440
as a Global North project

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:48.190
kind of acting on the Global South.

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.280
Instead this shows how development

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:51.320
was fundamentally actually,

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:52.610
a Global South project

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:54.640
from putting poverty eradication

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:58.450
at the center of the World
Bank as Mahbub ul Haq did

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:00.687
to the promotion of market
reforms in Sri Lanka

00:25:00.687 --> 00:25:04.410
and India as Lal Jayawardena
and Manmohan Singh did

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.380
to the invention of human development,

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:08.510
all of these have a history
that's fundamentally rooted

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:09.570
in the Global South.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:11.250
But specifically this has got

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:12.980
an embedded intellectual history.

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:14.540
One that sees the field's ideas,

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:18.180
not as emerging fully formed
from the minds of economists

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:19.670
but instead coming in response

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:21.360
to specific practical problems.

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:25.577
Kind of the friction
of theory and practice.

00:25:25.577 --> 00:25:29.110
All of these innovations in
development theory and practice

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:31.450
came actually out of
efforts to solve problems.

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:33.740
Theory doesn't descend into the world

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:34.810
but actually the reverse.

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:36.550
Efforts to solve practical problems

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:39.720
drove theoretical innovation
and finally this is,

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:41.820
a history of development that's embedded

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:43.450
in networks of family and friends.

00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:47.040
It focuses on the intellectual milieu

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.120
in which the six scholars operated.

00:25:49.120 --> 00:25:51.678
Both in their studies at Cambridge.

00:25:51.678 --> 00:25:54.310
It shows how the personal
connections of these scholars

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:55.143
shaped their work.

00:25:55.143 --> 00:25:56.860
The contacts they made with their teachers

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.380
and each other at Cambridge and beyond.

00:25:59.380 --> 00:26:01.680
All six of these were part of overlapping,

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.260
globe-spanning networks
that shaped their agendas.

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:07.410
It also touches, and
sort of being embedded

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:10.240
in what these economists
learned at home shall we say?

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:12.140
Mahbub ul Haq had two moments in the sun.

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:15.880
One in Pakistan in 1969

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:20.410
and when he announced
that even as a member

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:21.950
of the Pakistan Planning Commission

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:24.990
denounced policies that
he said countenanced

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:27.220
and even contributed to inequality

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:29.420
but really relies on a
decade's worth of work

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:31.250
by his wife, Khadija Haq

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:33.450
who had been interested
and by her own reports,

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:37.180
the only one interested
in economic inequality

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:39.500
among the Pakistani economists.

00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:41.321
Haq's second moment in the sun,

00:26:41.321 --> 00:26:44.600
the invention of the
Human Development Index

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:46.710
and the Human Development Report in 1990.

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:50.130
Also built on networks that
he had known for decades

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:52.180
including Amartya Sen but also

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.460
on the decade's worth
of work by Khadija Haq

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.503
to explore new development indicators.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:26:59.020
Other such situations are apparent

00:26:59.020 --> 00:27:01.000
for most of these scholars
which I could talk about

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.490
if people are interested.

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:03.323
I mean I think that,

00:27:03.323 --> 00:27:04.720
it shows that these embedded histories

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:07.950
help uncover multiple vectors
of power and privilege

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:11.430
that deepen our understanding
of how development's ideas

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:12.950
take shape and how they're applied.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:14.340
At least that's what I'm hoping to do

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:15.810
with this work Catherine.

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:17.660
- Thank you David and yeah,

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:19.688
I think that point is really interesting

00:27:19.688 --> 00:27:24.360
in terms of the influence
that Mahbub ul Haq's wife,

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:28.710
Khadija Haq had on his work
and yet it's hard to find

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:31.440
much information on that
which is part of the problem

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:33.690
and part of the value of
the work that you're doing.

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:34.980
So thank you so much.

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.440
And I also thought it was fascinating

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:42.400
that you've mentioned the
value these perspectives,

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:45.070
these early development
economists bring to the field

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:47.160
because they want to understand the world

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:48.790
in which they were raised.

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:50.490
What I hope we can get
to in our conversation

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:52.164
and again perhaps a narrative thread

00:27:52.164 --> 00:27:56.325
connecting these different
individuals is when you said

00:27:56.325 --> 00:27:58.190
that some of these scholars were saying

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:00.320
the wrong questions were being asked

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:02.320
and they wanted to use these tools

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.150
to pose and answer the right questions.

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:05.400
I wanna better understand

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:06.870
what were those questions being asked

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:09.090
and what were those questions
that they wanted asked?

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.600
So hopefully we can
return to that in a moment

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:14.250
but I want to bring
Amna into this as well.

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:16.980
So Amna, I know that your work has focused

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:18.910
on Akhtar Hameed Khan in part

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:21.460
who is very relevant to the
discussion we're having today.

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:24.955
So can you tell us again
more about the person,

00:28:24.955 --> 00:28:26.733
the path, the legacy.

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:29.620
- Thank you Catherine for the question

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:31.320
and thank you to the organizers

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:34.514
for bringing us all together
to have this conversation.

00:28:34.514 --> 00:28:38.210
So as Catherine mentioned,
I am working on.

00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:40.540
So my bigger project is on intersections

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:42.390
between global biopolitics

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:45.360
and postcolonial development
and one aspect of it

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:47.625
is thinking about how rural development

00:28:47.625 --> 00:28:51.600
is imagined and practiced in the 1960s.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:53.290
So two big points that I'd like to make

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:54.630
and I think this brings

00:28:54.630 --> 00:28:58.070
some of what we've been
speaking about together.

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:01.310
So firstly I think
we've been talking about

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:03.400
the development of development economics

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:05.970
as it grows to a scientific
field of knowledge

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:07.390
but I'd also like us to think about

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:09.310
how it was shaped by actors

00:29:09.310 --> 00:29:11.640
who we wouldn't traditionally
peg as economists

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:14.390
or even academics for that matter

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:16.170
and to get us thinking a little bit more

00:29:16.170 --> 00:29:17.380
about the intersections

00:29:17.380 --> 00:29:21.157
between developmental practice
and academic knowledge

00:29:21.157 --> 00:29:24.220
and how those inform each
other in different ways.

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:25.960
And secondly, I think what I'd also like

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:28.140
is to start thinking about the geography

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:30.250
of developmental foreign practice

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:34.010
and David mentioned these six
economists from South Asia

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:35.780
who converge at Cambridge

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:37.800
and had these exciting trajectories

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:40.400
across both the Global
North and the Global South

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:45.130
but I'd also like to insert
geography from the Global South

00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:47.140
as particular sites,

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:50.450
as crucial sites actually
of developmental thought

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:54.653
which put forward these models
of developmental practice.

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:59.030
So Akhtar Hameed Khan, just a
very brief biographical note,

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:03.150
he was born in 1914 in Agra
in what is present day India.

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:07.717
So roughly contemporaneous
to Arthur Lewis.

00:30:07.717 --> 00:30:11.430
And he is not trained
as a social scientist

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:12.900
but he is rather,

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:16.520
in today's speak would be a technocrat

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:17.817
but he worked for a little while

00:30:17.817 --> 00:30:21.094
for the Indian Civil Service
under British colonial rule

00:30:21.094 --> 00:30:25.797
where in the early 1940s
he was posted to Bengal

00:30:25.797 --> 00:30:29.876
and in 1943 he dramatically resigned

00:30:29.876 --> 00:30:33.010
from the Indian Civil
Service as a sign of protest

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:35.730
against British colonial
policies during the Bengal famine

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:39.000
of 1943 and then for the next few years

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:43.090
he worked as a school principal in Comilla

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:45.840
in what is present day Bangladesh

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:48.980
and then in 1958 he's
appointed as a Director

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:52.329
for the newly formed Pakistan
Academy of Rural Development

00:30:52.329 --> 00:30:53.350
in Comilla.

00:30:53.350 --> 00:30:57.102
So present-day Bangladesh but
what was then east Pakistan

00:30:57.102 --> 00:30:59.860
and what he developed
there was today known

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:01.617
as the Comilla model of development

00:31:01.617 --> 00:31:04.343
and I'll speak very
briefly about the model

00:31:04.343 --> 00:31:06.440
because there are many aspects to it

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:08.700
but it was based on four operators.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:10.345
The formation of list of operators

00:31:10.345 --> 00:31:11.850
and five participatory decision making

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:14.034
in which villages would come together

00:31:14.034 --> 00:31:16.550
and decide on how to save and invest

00:31:16.550 --> 00:31:18.620
for agricultural development.

00:31:18.620 --> 00:31:22.460
There was also a focus on
labor-intensive public work

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:24.501
in which again, the corporate members

00:31:24.501 --> 00:31:27.640
would work together on
projects such as irrigation,

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:30.940
rural works, drainage, et
cetera and a lot of these,

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:32.700
while they were drawing on older forms

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:35.720
of thinking about cooperative models,

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.682
what distinguished Comilla in the 1960s

00:31:38.682 --> 00:31:40.720
were two primary things.

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:43.400
Firstly was Akhtar
Hameed Khan's insistence

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:47.193
on participatory and
bottom-up decision making

00:31:47.193 --> 00:31:50.607
through mobilizing the
community and its resources.

00:31:50.607 --> 00:31:53.020
And this was different from earlier forms

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:55.060
of US-led community development

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:57.110
that were pioneered in the 1950s

00:31:57.110 --> 00:32:02.110
which sort of inserted
outsiders to sort of teach,

00:32:02.373 --> 00:32:04.700
as in how to you know,

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:07.220
know how and how to practice various forms

00:32:07.220 --> 00:32:10.370
of agricultural practices.

00:32:10.370 --> 00:32:12.685
So while this was not always,

00:32:12.685 --> 00:32:13.740
and you know, we can
speak more about this.

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.770
It wasn't always so similar to practice.

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:19.282
In theory what was conceptualized

00:32:19.282 --> 00:32:24.282
was sort of two-way sharing of knowledge

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:27.420
and the learning process in
which peasants were leading

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:29.433
their community's development.

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:34.260
And secondly, what made
Akhtar Hameed Khan's thinking

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:35.980
and the project at Comilla interesting

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:38.250
was its focus early on,

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:40.760
from the early 1960s on women

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:43.020
and the household as what they called

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:44.540
not as objects of development

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:47.810
but as active agents of
their own development.

00:32:47.810 --> 00:32:49.440
So while we were seeing
quite a lot of focus

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.130
in the 1960s on you know,

00:32:51.130 --> 00:32:54.010
development of spectacle
and as Ravi mentioned,

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:57.740
in Ghana there was a lot of
focus on industrialization.

00:32:57.740 --> 00:33:00.637
So the steel mill or
the dam or what have you

00:33:00.637 --> 00:33:02.080
and this was being replicated

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:05.560
in a lot of other force
influential countries as well.

00:33:05.560 --> 00:33:06.940
What the model at Comilla did

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:09.483
was to focus also on social norms,

00:33:09.483 --> 00:33:12.190
gender norms and reproductive practices

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:17.190
as aspects of developmental
thinking and practice.

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.860
So through broad sort of
sociological knowledge

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:21.570
that was being produced at Comilla

00:33:21.570 --> 00:33:24.353
and the projects that were being signed,

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:28.860
women were slowly incorporated
into developmental thinking

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:29.693
as key actors

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.220
and not just actors through you know,

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:33.942
whose reproduction must be controlled.

00:33:33.942 --> 00:33:34.960
Although that was a big part of it

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:38.300
and I can speak more about
it if anyone's interested

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:42.900
but also as economic agents who required

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:45.163
technical and vocational training.

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:48.060
So I think in this way,
Comilla also provides

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:50.670
an early glimpse into
the ways in which women

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:54.160
were slowly being linked
into economic development

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:58.060
and thought and practice
and this would later,

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:01.510
forcefully I think
inform academic knowledge

00:34:01.510 --> 00:34:02.910
in development economics as a field

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:04.523
and what is today known as

00:34:04.523 --> 00:34:08.980
the women in development
approaches in the 1970s

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:12.040
which really came to the
fore and global consciousness

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:13.560
at that point.

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:15.900
With some ministry
economists such as Deske Jen

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:17.613
and Ester Boserup.

00:34:17.613 --> 00:34:19.790
They were forcefully theorizing

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:22.166
the links between economic productivity,

00:34:22.166 --> 00:34:23.580
development and gender

00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:27.310
but I think focusing on Comilla provides

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:31.080
us an interesting glimpse
into not just how you know,

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:33.175
academic theory effects practice

00:34:33.175 --> 00:34:35.467
but how developmental practice informs

00:34:35.467 --> 00:34:38.613
the formation of development
economics as a field.

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:43.980
- Thank you Amna and I think
your question has inspired.

00:34:43.980 --> 00:34:46.490
Or sorry, your remarks have inspired

00:34:46.490 --> 00:34:47.600
a great question from our audience

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.380
which is also a question
that that I wanted to pose.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:55.000
So you talked a lot about
Akhtar Hameed Khan's impact

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:55.833
on development.

00:34:55.833 --> 00:34:58.870
Including this participatory
decision making

00:34:58.870 --> 00:35:02.250
as well as women as agents of development.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:04.620
Staying on that theme
of women for a moment

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:08.220
and specifically women
in development economics,

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:10.200
one of our attendees asks,

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:11.610
could the panelists shed light

00:35:11.610 --> 00:35:14.550
on the missing mothers
of development economics?

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:18.690
Why do we value less the
work of Joan Robinson,

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:22.020
Krishna Bharadwaj and others

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:23.580
relative to their male counterparts.

00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:27.810
So Joan Robinson came
up earlier and you know,

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:28.917
I think that's a great question

00:35:28.917 --> 00:35:32.570
and actually one I was hoping
to pose as well as a followup.

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:34.433
Ravi, is that something you could tell us

00:35:34.433 --> 00:35:36.073
a little bit more about?

00:35:36.073 --> 00:35:39.590
- Sure I can say a few words and you know,

00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:41.950
David said that was it the first,

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:45.920
first lecture that
Amartya and Mahbub went to

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:48.446
was Joan Robinson's lecture.

00:35:48.446 --> 00:35:53.446
Now Joan Robinson, she was
born in 1903 and died in 1983.

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:59.296
So I suppose in the 50s she
would've been in her 50s

00:35:59.296 --> 00:36:01.080
at the time.

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:03.709
As an undergraduate in Cambridge myself,

00:36:03.709 --> 00:36:07.773
she was a presence up there so to speak.

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:11.750
But then as a young faculty
member at Cambridge,

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:14.533
as the equivalent of assistant
professor at Cambridge,

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.920
again she was very much a
presence in the common room,

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:19.963
in the faculty room.

00:36:20.824 --> 00:36:24.016
Arguing and debating and so on.

00:36:24.016 --> 00:36:26.990
Joan Robinson is actually known for two,

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:28.920
at least two things in economics.

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:31.380
One is her contribution to economic theory

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:36.380
and in particular her analysis
in the 1930s of monopoly

00:36:37.903 --> 00:36:42.903
and in fact she coined, she
coined the word monopsony

00:36:43.940 --> 00:36:47.260
which is where the buyer
of a product has power.

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:49.380
Monopoly is where the seller
of a product has power,

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:51.297
monopsony is where the
buyer of a product has power

00:36:51.297 --> 00:36:53.490
and the key product for where the buyer

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:57.370
may have power is of course labor.

00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:00.500
And we're seeing now with
platforms and so on so forth,

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:02.549
the whole question of monopsony,

00:37:02.549 --> 00:37:07.010
the power that buyers have
of labor has come to the fore

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:09.602
and it was Joan Robinson
who actually did the first

00:37:09.602 --> 00:37:13.710
rigorous analysis of
this back in the 1930s.

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:15.640
And there are other major
contributions she made

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:18.480
to economic theory at the time.

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.160
But relevant to this discussion,

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:24.180
she also was very much engaged
in development question,

00:37:24.180 --> 00:37:27.320
the questions of economic
development and two things

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:29.210
I would point out.

00:37:29.210 --> 00:37:34.210
In the 1970s, she became
very closely engaged

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:36.900
with a great Indian economist.

00:37:36.900 --> 00:37:39.043
He wasn't at Cambridge at the time David.

00:37:39.043 --> 00:37:42.100
He was an early generation,
he was at the LSE,

00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:43.820
his name was K. N. Raj.

00:37:43.820 --> 00:37:46.700
K. N. Raj who founded something called

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:49.660
the Centre for Development
Studies in Kerala.

00:37:49.660 --> 00:37:51.560
And was in fact part of developing

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:56.560
the so-called Kerala model
of social expenditures,

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.567
attention to women's development

00:37:59.567 --> 00:38:02.410
and so on and so forth
and it was in conjunction

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:04.150
with the left-led governments

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:06.697
which dominated Kerala for a long time,

00:38:06.697 --> 00:38:08.799
if you like that was the economic wing

00:38:08.799 --> 00:38:12.410
of that whole development
and Joan Robinson

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:14.700
had a very close
relationship with K. N. Raj

00:38:14.700 --> 00:38:17.810
and she would visit the
Centre for Development Studies

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:20.840
in Thiruvananthapuram
on a very regular basis

00:38:20.840 --> 00:38:24.290
and engage with policymakers
and so on at the time.

00:38:24.290 --> 00:38:26.140
So that is one area.

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:29.330
The other area where I
think it perhaps we can have

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:31.940
more questions about
her is that she actually

00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:36.940
in the 1960s engaged a lot
with developments in China

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:41.390
and with the cultural revolution
and North Korea and so on

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:43.597
and the argument is that she was,

00:38:44.629 --> 00:38:47.230
is that she was too
uncritical of the developments

00:38:47.230 --> 00:38:51.467
that were going on in
the cultural revolution

00:38:51.467 --> 00:38:55.350
and also actually in North Korea

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:59.010
where she gave glowing
reports of developments

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:00.700
in North Korea.

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:03.180
So there's been a debate
and a discussion on that

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:04.600
in the literature.

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:08.210
Harking back to people in the 1930s

00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:12.620
who went to the Soviet
Union and didn't see

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:14.590
below the surface, et cetera.

00:39:14.590 --> 00:39:18.690
So you know, she's an
absolute giant in our field

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:23.690
and in my view, for her
contributions to economics,

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:25.650
she should've won the Nobel Prize.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:29.750
Plain and simple, no question,
no question about it at all.

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:32.750
- So Ravi, as you mentioned
she is a giant in the field

00:39:32.750 --> 00:39:36.240
and you know, this question
from the audience is,

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:39.160
why is her work perhaps valued less

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:42.523
than the work of some of
her male counterparts.

00:39:42.523 --> 00:39:44.280
Or perhaps put another way,

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:46.530
why didn't she win the Nobel Prize?

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:49.500
Is this something perhaps Rohini
that you wanna comment on?

00:39:49.500 --> 00:39:51.500
Just in terms of connecting this to

00:39:51.500 --> 00:39:54.080
some of the broader
themes of this symposium?

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:54.913
- Sure, happy to do so.

00:39:54.913 --> 00:39:58.870
Let me, you know I
recently wrote a piece on,

00:39:58.870 --> 00:40:01.080
in fact this started off perhaps

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:04.270
with Cambridge School of
Economics, Alfred Marshall.

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:09.260
And his extreme misogyny
where he was very opposed

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:12.530
to women getting degrees
but also seemed to have

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:15.810
made very great use of his wife,

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:19.470
Mary Paley Marshall's great
talents as an economist.

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:22.760
She actually was a
coauthor on a book with him

00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:25.544
on the economics of
industry which preceded

00:40:25.544 --> 00:40:27.670
the famous Principles of Economics

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:31.170
and Marshall very
explicitly sought to keep it

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.110
out of print once his book was published.

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:36.840
But where I wanted to go with that is I,

00:40:36.840 --> 00:40:39.740
and as David in fact helped me realize,

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:42.090
Cambridge economists wrote
very detailed letters

00:40:42.090 --> 00:40:45.163
to each other, notes and
they're all archived.

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:51.070
I got email from a young
undergraduate at Yale

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:52.940
who had just finished last year.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:56.111
She was one of the top
students of her year.

00:40:56.111 --> 00:40:58.660
Saying oh,

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:00.930
I read something and I'll
read it out in a second.

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:04.220
Footnote 19, and this is
something I heard all the time

00:41:04.220 --> 00:41:05.883
from my contemporaries at Yale.

00:41:07.198 --> 00:41:11.542
So the quote was from
Marshall who described,

00:41:11.542 --> 00:41:14.450
he put forward a set of arguments

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:16.873
to the Cambridge Senate on why
women shouldn't do economics

00:41:16.873 --> 00:41:19.120
and one of them was he said,

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:20.940
while he felt the questions asked by women

00:41:20.940 --> 00:41:23.020
generally relate to
the lecture or bookwork

00:41:23.020 --> 00:41:26.010
or else to practical
problems such as poor relief,

00:41:26.010 --> 00:41:27.980
men who have attended fewer lectures,

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:30.380
read fewer books and perhaps likely

00:41:30.380 --> 00:41:32.840
to obtain less marks in examinations

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:35.890
are more apt to ask questions
showing mental initiative

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:38.290
and giving promise of
richer work in the future.

00:41:39.350 --> 00:41:41.980
And she felt, she was always at Yale

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:45.120
put into the category
of the industrious girl

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:48.010
who came at the top of
her class but you know,

00:41:48.010 --> 00:41:51.200
perhaps was not asking
the smartest questions.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.440
So to link that back to what Ravi said

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:57.040
and why the, and many
women didn't get credit,

00:41:57.040 --> 00:41:58.270
I think that's part of it.

00:41:58.270 --> 00:42:01.130
So if you look to the long arc of history,

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:02.560
as Ravi said you know,

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:06.750
Joan Robinson identified monopsony.

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:09.890
Another person whose name came
up earlier was Beatrice Webb.

00:42:09.890 --> 00:42:12.350
She's the person who identified the word

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:14.238
collective bargaining in her book

00:42:14.238 --> 00:42:15.450
on the theory of poor critters.

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:19.240
Which was another book that
Marshall actively told her

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:21.080
not to write because he told her

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:22.710
she should focus on women's problems,

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:24.163
not on collective bargaining.

00:42:25.420 --> 00:42:30.157
And so you know, I think
perhaps a very poignant example

00:42:31.090 --> 00:42:33.020
of what happened to many of these women

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:35.560
and what continues on is actually in

00:42:35.560 --> 00:42:38.040
a letter that Alfred Marshall's wife,

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:42.263
Mary Paley Marshall wrote to
Joan Robinson in the 1930s

00:42:42.263 --> 00:42:45.110
when her book on monopsony came out.

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:48.370
She was also the godmother
to Joan Robinson's daughter

00:42:48.370 --> 00:42:49.347
and she wrote,

00:42:49.347 --> 00:42:52.387
"Thank you for helping
lift off the reproach cast

00:42:52.387 --> 00:42:54.240
"on the economic woman."

00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:58.333
And I think this is
something that we hear about

00:42:58.333 --> 00:42:59.730
when it comes to race,
when it comes to gender

00:42:59.730 --> 00:43:03.712
that the canons of economics
were often written,

00:43:03.712 --> 00:43:08.090
not just with the notion of
someone who doesn't really exist

00:43:08.090 --> 00:43:11.340
but also by someone who
had a very strong notion

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:15.410
of what work should be canonized

00:43:15.410 --> 00:43:17.550
and I think we are still
seeing the aftermath of that.

00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:22.550
Not just in women or economists
from the Global South

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:28.030
or members of certain social groups

00:43:28.030 --> 00:43:29.980
not getting the recognition they should.

00:43:29.980 --> 00:43:32.330
But I think even more broadly than that,

00:43:32.330 --> 00:43:35.220
a narrowing of the
questions that we are asking

00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:39.630
in development economics or
economics more generally.

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:40.780
- Absolutely, thanks Rohini

00:43:40.780 --> 00:43:42.760
and we have some great
questions coming in.

00:43:42.760 --> 00:43:44.360
It's actually hard to choose

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:46.780
which is a good position to
be in but keep them coming.

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:48.670
I wanna start with this question

00:43:48.670 --> 00:43:50.640
which I thought was a great one.

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:52.560
In part because it's sort of looking ahead

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:53.600
and where do we go from here.

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:56.617
So this is an attendee saying,

00:43:56.617 --> 00:43:57.567
"A lot of ideas

00:43:57.567 --> 00:43:59.847
"on the decolonization
of development economics

00:43:59.847 --> 00:44:03.437
"can be found in journals
produced in developing countries

00:44:03.437 --> 00:44:06.817
"which are not given
prominence in the wider world.

00:44:06.817 --> 00:44:08.817
"What can be done to harness these ideas

00:44:08.817 --> 00:44:10.640
"in the field of development economics?"

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:15.640
So let's say there are economists
right now who you know,

00:44:17.240 --> 00:44:19.890
50 years from now there will be a webinar

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:24.510
discussing their legacy but
perhaps much of their writing

00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:26.160
is not getting the kind of attention

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:27.970
that it should globally.

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:29.720
So what can be done to change that?

00:44:30.883 --> 00:44:31.813
Thoughts on that question?

00:44:37.510 --> 00:44:39.670
And if not, plenty of
others are rolling in.

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:40.503
But go ahead Ravi.

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:46.480
- Well, so let me.

00:44:46.480 --> 00:44:50.850
Let me perhaps take a
somewhat more positive

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:53.457
and forward-looking approach to this

00:44:53.457 --> 00:44:55.000
and say that if we go back

00:44:55.000 --> 00:44:58.473
to the time of Joan Robinson and so on,

00:44:58.473 --> 00:45:03.250
there really was only Cambridge
if you see what I mean

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:04.890
in terms of economics.

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:07.780
Very little in the way
of general communication

00:45:07.780 --> 00:45:08.740
and so on and so forth

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:11.673
and it was very restricted
channels of communication

00:45:11.673 --> 00:45:16.673
and the economic journal was
a preserve of a few people

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:18.443
and so on.

00:45:19.383 --> 00:45:23.450
I think what gives hope is
that situation has changed

00:45:23.450 --> 00:45:26.380
because of new technology
and so on and so forth.

00:45:26.380 --> 00:45:30.040
So in fact some of these,
some of the perspectives.

00:45:30.040 --> 00:45:34.228
That one would have lost 60, 70 years ago,

00:45:34.228 --> 00:45:37.775
at least there's a prospect
that they will be brought

00:45:37.775 --> 00:45:40.545
to the center stage.

00:45:40.545 --> 00:45:43.210
That's what I would, what I would say.

00:45:43.210 --> 00:45:46.270
There's of course always the
issue in all academic things

00:45:46.270 --> 00:45:47.770
and we have a bunch of academics here

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:51.860
in terms of the reification
of certain journals

00:45:51.860 --> 00:45:56.860
and certain whatever but
that's the academic frame.

00:45:58.990 --> 00:45:59.823
- Thanks Ravi.

00:46:00.780 --> 00:46:02.630
Amna, did you wanna jump in?

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:04.000
- I think we all seem to think about

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:06.180
the terms of engagement in which,

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:08.470
well just to use the catchall phrase,

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:11.190
the Global South is engaging,

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:13.450
is being engaged with when it comes to

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:16.180
the field of economics and
many other academic fields

00:46:16.180 --> 00:46:17.260
where well,

00:46:17.260 --> 00:46:19.330
to use the shorthand, local scholars

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:23.490
are seen as research assistants
or information gatherers

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:25.530
and their insights aren't seen

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:28.770
as being theoretically
sound enough to actually,

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:30.300
they're just there for giving us

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:31.650
local knowledge and practice

00:46:31.650 --> 00:46:33.640
and then it's someone who's sitting in

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:35.360
the Global North who will you know,

00:46:35.360 --> 00:46:38.360
fantastically come up with
all these great ideas.

00:46:38.360 --> 00:46:39.870
So I think maybe the smaller things

00:46:39.870 --> 00:46:43.380
like coauthorship might
be a good way forward

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:44.790
in recognizing that it's,

00:46:44.790 --> 00:46:47.590
and sort of trying to make it

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:49.861
a little bit less hierarchical

00:46:49.861 --> 00:46:52.061
in terms of how knowledge
is being produced.

00:46:54.123 --> 00:46:55.045
- Thanks Amna.

00:46:55.045 --> 00:46:57.080
I wanna go to another great question here.

00:46:57.080 --> 00:46:58.557
So someone asks,

00:46:58.557 --> 00:47:01.437
"If one wants to decolonize development,

00:47:01.437 --> 00:47:05.297
"then it cannot simply be about
a different kind of person

00:47:05.297 --> 00:47:06.530
"engaging in development."

00:47:06.530 --> 00:47:10.440
So we've been talking a lot
about who these thinkers were

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:12.450
and are and the importance of diversity.

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:13.610
But this person adds,

00:47:13.610 --> 00:47:16.930
it must be about using the
tools, institutional power

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:19.400
and methods to create decolonization.

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:20.470
So the question is,

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:24.030
can you distinguish the thinkers
some of you have brought up

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:26.810
by clarifying which
ones sought development

00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:29.490
of a single type and which ones sought

00:47:29.490 --> 00:47:31.263
actual decolonization?

00:47:32.111 --> 00:47:35.730
So this gets a little
bit deeper into the work,

00:47:35.730 --> 00:47:38.530
the questions they were posing,
the work they were doing

00:47:38.530 --> 00:47:43.530
and to what extent
decolonization of development,

00:47:43.940 --> 00:47:46.120
not just the field of
development economics

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:48.290
was part of their legacy.

00:47:48.290 --> 00:47:50.710
So maybe our panelists wanna jump in

00:47:50.710 --> 00:47:52.320
and shed a bit more light

00:47:52.320 --> 00:47:54.570
on the people you talked
about in that sense.

00:47:56.388 --> 00:47:57.753
I thought it was a great question, David.

00:47:58.845 --> 00:47:59.678
- That is a great question.

00:47:59.678 --> 00:48:01.850
I feel like for the six
who I'm looking at there

00:48:01.850 --> 00:48:04.414
are kind of all over the map on this one.

00:48:04.414 --> 00:48:06.180
So two of them when,

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:10.810
remarkable accolades in the
Academy of the Global North

00:48:10.810 --> 00:48:11.973
by Gwati and Sen.

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:15.160
I asked then where he learned
his development economics

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:17.100
and he said there
actually is no such thing

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:18.200
as development economics.

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:19.350
There's only economics.

00:48:20.500 --> 00:48:24.500
And so clearly that his move
is decolonizing in some ways

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:25.400
and not in others.

00:48:26.832 --> 00:48:30.760
One of the things that struck
me is how most of them,

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:32.740
most of the people who I'm looking at

00:48:32.740 --> 00:48:37.030
got really deeply engaged
and devoted tremendous energy

00:48:37.030 --> 00:48:41.310
to working in international institutions

00:48:41.310 --> 00:48:43.760
and to creating those institutions.

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:47.300
So Manmohan Singh for instance
who's often thought of,

00:48:47.300 --> 00:48:50.024
well rightly thought of as one of the,

00:48:50.024 --> 00:48:55.024
the innovators in liberalizing
the Indian economy in 1991,

00:48:56.016 --> 00:48:57.805
his prior job was actually working for

00:48:57.805 --> 00:49:00.400
the socialist leader of Tanzania,

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:01.710
former leader of Tanzania,

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:04.420
Julius Nyerere in a project
called The South Commission

00:49:04.420 --> 00:49:06.850
which was to create a thinktank

00:49:06.850 --> 00:49:10.532
and a kind of nexus for
intellectual political life

00:49:10.532 --> 00:49:13.853
by and for the Global South.

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:17.200
A number of the people I'm looking at

00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:19.970
worked at UN agencies
at one point or another.

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:22.230
Mahbub ul Haq and Khadija
Haq together created

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:26.350
a series of smaller, more
ephemeral organizations

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:29.750
and so they were very active I think

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:34.750
and devoted extensive energy
to institution building

00:49:36.710 --> 00:49:40.680
or working within
institutions to redirect them

00:49:41.829 --> 00:49:43.810
and so I think that's one of
the places that we can see it

00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:46.450
is it actually requires perhaps

00:49:46.450 --> 00:49:48.550
leaving the academy or
at least in the cases

00:49:48.550 --> 00:49:50.940
I'm looking at, those
who stuck in the academy

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:57.113
tended to be doing conventional
economics brilliantly well.

00:49:58.143 --> 00:50:03.143
But not rethinking all of the
premises along the dimensions

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:05.860
that we're talking about here.

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:10.180
- A very interesting point
and partly because perhaps,

00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:12.530
while remaining in the world of academia,

00:50:12.530 --> 00:50:14.470
those theories and concepts

00:50:14.470 --> 00:50:16.210
weren't bumping up against realities

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:18.480
that can change your thinking.

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:20.040
Other thoughts from our panelists

00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:22.190
in response to this great question?

00:50:22.190 --> 00:50:24.860
Otherwise we have others coming in, but.

00:50:24.860 --> 00:50:26.780
Amna did you wanna say something on this?

00:50:26.780 --> 00:50:28.950
- Yeah I'll just have, this is,

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:30.430
she delivered such a great question

00:50:30.430 --> 00:50:33.220
and I think it requires more
thought and exploitation

00:50:33.220 --> 00:50:35.880
but what I will say I think from,

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:38.810
and I think Ravi also mentioned this

00:50:38.810 --> 00:50:40.690
is that a lot of the thinkers
that we're looking at,

00:50:40.690 --> 00:50:43.500
they're bumping up against politics

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:45.920
and in many instances they're working with

00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:47.160
a authoritarian regimes.

00:50:47.160 --> 00:50:50.840
So in my case Akhtar Hameed
Khan is working with Ayub Khan.

00:50:50.840 --> 00:50:52.850
Mahbub ul Haq is as David mentioned

00:50:52.850 --> 00:50:57.850
is also an advisor in Ayub Khan's regime.

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:01.010
So while they're having conflicts,

00:51:01.010 --> 00:51:02.890
I think they are more wedded to ideas

00:51:02.890 --> 00:51:07.780
of post-colonial development
than decolonization.

00:51:07.780 --> 00:51:10.810
If we were to think about decolonization

00:51:10.810 --> 00:51:12.740
as a project of self determination.

00:51:12.740 --> 00:51:16.780
Maybe someone like Rehman
Sobhan who David looks at

00:51:16.780 --> 00:51:19.830
and who is termed as a freedom fighter

00:51:19.830 --> 00:51:22.260
in the liberation of Bangladesh,

00:51:22.260 --> 00:51:23.700
I think maybe if we focus on him

00:51:23.700 --> 00:51:26.454
we can think more about decolonization.

00:51:26.454 --> 00:51:29.680
But I think some of the
examples that we've given,

00:51:29.680 --> 00:51:32.320
there is definitely a link between

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:34.902
post-colonial development,
authoritarianism

00:51:34.902 --> 00:51:37.393
and development economics as a field.

00:51:38.980 --> 00:51:40.400
- Thanks Amna.

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:41.800
As we have more questions coming in,

00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:44.558
I wanna return to a couple
things that were said

00:51:44.558 --> 00:51:46.430
that I think might help
build some connections

00:51:46.430 --> 00:51:48.950
between these people who
have been brought up.

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:52.420
So David, that actually starts
with something you mentioned.

00:51:52.420 --> 00:51:54.220
That some of the economists
you're focused on

00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:57.040
who were together at
Cambridge during the same time

00:51:57.040 --> 00:51:59.400
talked about how the wrong
questions were being asked

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:04.070
and they wanted to change the
nature of the conversation

00:52:04.070 --> 00:52:05.710
and pose and answer the right questions.

00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:08.000
So can you expand in a little more detail

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:09.250
on what you mean by that?

00:52:10.930 --> 00:52:13.260
- I would just take for starters,

00:52:13.260 --> 00:52:15.740
one of the questions they
all were engaged with

00:52:15.740 --> 00:52:18.270
in different ways in
different times was inequality

00:52:18.270 --> 00:52:21.633
and here we could look at this
in two different dimensions.

00:52:21.633 --> 00:52:24.580
On a domestic level and
at an international level

00:52:24.580 --> 00:52:27.422
and so at the domestic level,

00:52:27.422 --> 00:52:31.140
we're talking in a moment where inequality

00:52:31.140 --> 00:52:35.120
is seen as a perhaps
unfortunate in some cases

00:52:35.120 --> 00:52:37.770
but always necessary
byproduct of economic growth

00:52:37.770 --> 00:52:39.520
that would sort of solve itself.

00:52:39.520 --> 00:52:41.230
This is the famous Kuznets curve

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:44.403
from Simon Kuznets who Rohini mentioned.

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:48.323
Whereas I think we find
in all of these scholars

00:52:48.323 --> 00:52:50.170
a determination,

00:52:50.170 --> 00:52:52.840
even though they had different
prescriptions shall we say,

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:57.210
determination to address
questions of economic inequality

00:52:57.210 --> 00:52:59.040
at the domestic level.

00:52:59.040 --> 00:53:01.270
Amartya Sen I think in
particular was focused

00:53:01.270 --> 00:53:04.080
on the intersections
especially in his later work,

00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:06.980
the intersection of economic inequality

00:53:06.980 --> 00:53:10.500
and other structures
of domestic inequality,

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:12.323
caste and gender among them.

00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:16.712
At the second level of
global economic power,

00:53:16.712 --> 00:53:21.480
it was too easy for I think for economists

00:53:21.480 --> 00:53:24.286
just to think of the world as made up of,

00:53:24.286 --> 00:53:29.030
of nations with equal
rights and therefore perhaps

00:53:29.030 --> 00:53:32.340
equal entry points into
the global economy.

00:53:32.340 --> 00:53:34.750
Whereas I think people
like Jagdish Bhagwati,

00:53:34.750 --> 00:53:36.320
Manmohan Singh, Lal Jayawardena

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:38.990
were very focused on the nuts and bolts

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:42.203
of global economic inequality and worked,

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:44.950
Bhagwati stayed mostly in academia

00:53:44.950 --> 00:53:46.830
but Singh and Jayawardena

00:53:46.830 --> 00:53:50.210
worked in the trenches
at the IMF actually,

00:53:50.210 --> 00:53:53.380
at the UN Conference for
Trade and Development,

00:53:53.380 --> 00:53:56.980
at the South Commission
to try and find ways

00:53:56.980 --> 00:54:01.980
to address, to redress
global economic inequality.

00:54:02.889 --> 00:54:05.766
So those, if I had one
question that would be it.

00:54:05.766 --> 00:54:07.290
Albeit in two parts.

00:54:07.290 --> 00:54:09.865
- Yeah that's really helpful, thank you.

00:54:09.865 --> 00:54:11.280
I wanna bring in another
question from the audience.

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:14.380
Actually two questions
related to Arthur Lewis

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:18.053
and so perhaps Ravi or
Gerry wanna jump in this.

00:54:19.020 --> 00:54:19.980
Here are the two questions.

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:23.810
The first is, in relation to the tension

00:54:23.810 --> 00:54:25.760
between economics and politics

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:27.210
which has come up a few times.

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:30.020
Would you say that Arthur
Lewis covered aspects of this

00:54:30.020 --> 00:54:32.360
in his book, "Theory of Economic Growth"?

00:54:32.360 --> 00:54:33.310
That's one question.

00:54:33.310 --> 00:54:35.104
And then the other which I'll throw in.

00:54:35.104 --> 00:54:38.750
When teaching I would tell
students that Arthur Lewis

00:54:38.750 --> 00:54:41.990
never claimed the marginal
product of labor was zero,

00:54:41.990 --> 00:54:44.230
he argued that in non-capitalist sectors,

00:54:44.230 --> 00:54:46.520
the marginal product of
a laborer could be zero

00:54:46.520 --> 00:54:48.100
as compensated by greater effort

00:54:48.100 --> 00:54:50.210
by the rest of the household unit.

00:54:50.210 --> 00:54:52.580
Why was that insight
so often misunderstood?

00:54:52.580 --> 00:54:56.210
So two questions directly pointed at our

00:54:57.110 --> 00:54:59.560
Arthur Lewis experts here

00:54:59.560 --> 00:55:01.330
and I wonder if you can react to those

00:55:01.330 --> 00:55:03.960
and perhaps even shed
a little bit more light

00:55:03.960 --> 00:55:06.890
about Arthur Lewis and
his legacy in doing so.

00:55:06.890 --> 00:55:09.890
So Ravi or Gerry, who
wants to jump in first?

00:55:09.890 --> 00:55:10.723
Ravi?
- Gerry, why don't you

00:55:10.723 --> 00:55:12.253
go first, then I'll follow up?

00:55:14.927 --> 00:55:15.760
You're muted.

00:55:17.360 --> 00:55:21.360
- I was thinking that this
was more in your valley wick

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:23.390
and you should go first.

00:55:23.390 --> 00:55:24.760
So please do.
- Yeah.

00:55:24.760 --> 00:55:26.273
Sure, sure.

00:55:26.273 --> 00:55:27.106
So the answer to the first question,

00:55:27.106 --> 00:55:29.200
actually I don't think the
theory of economic growth

00:55:29.200 --> 00:55:32.790
was directed to that sort of question

00:55:32.790 --> 00:55:36.967
of the intricate
interaction between politics

00:55:36.967 --> 00:55:38.853
and economics and so on.

00:55:40.390 --> 00:55:44.180
In fact, it was our, it was
Lewis' politics in West Africa

00:55:44.180 --> 00:55:48.410
which sort of took head on
the question of the economy.

00:55:49.332 --> 00:55:51.020
And you know, and he was criticized

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:52.640
for a lot of his theorizing and that

00:55:52.640 --> 00:55:54.860
and his analysis and that.

00:55:54.860 --> 00:55:56.740
But it was his attempt to make sense

00:55:56.740 --> 00:55:59.030
of what he had seen and think.

00:55:59.030 --> 00:56:02.373
So just another little quote from Lewis.

00:56:03.330 --> 00:56:07.720
He's talking about the
development plan in 1957 and 1958.

00:56:07.720 --> 00:56:09.600
And this was the exchange with Nkrumah

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:13.433
which basically led to him
resigning as an advisor.

00:56:14.810 --> 00:56:15.643
He says,

00:56:16.488 --> 00:56:18.127
"It is not easy to make
a good development plan

00:56:18.127 --> 00:56:22.177
"for 100 million pounds
if the Prime Minister

00:56:22.177 --> 00:56:25.667
"insists on inserting 18 million
pounds of his pet schemes

00:56:25.667 --> 00:56:27.687
"of a sort which neither
develop the country

00:56:27.687 --> 00:56:30.782
"nor increase the comfort of the people."

00:56:30.782 --> 00:56:32.240
Okay and that's a very sort of

00:56:32.240 --> 00:56:36.000
technocratic economist-type
of perspective.

00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:38.210
Of course Nkrumah's response was,

00:56:38.210 --> 00:56:40.360
the reason why the whole
show is on the road

00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:42.870
is because of these 18 million pounds

00:56:42.870 --> 00:56:46.510
that I am using in this way or that way

00:56:46.510 --> 00:56:48.803
to keep different groups happy.

00:56:49.679 --> 00:56:52.370
And I guess we, and
certainly I as an economist

00:56:52.370 --> 00:56:57.370
haven't resolved that
tension in my mind at all.

00:56:58.990 --> 00:57:00.000
So that was that point

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:01.730
and then the second point is
a much more technical point

00:57:01.730 --> 00:57:03.340
so I'm not sure we should
necessarily get into it

00:57:03.340 --> 00:57:05.293
in great detail.

00:57:05.293 --> 00:57:06.126
- It's okay.
- But it's a great point

00:57:06.126 --> 00:57:09.710
and I actually, going back
to one of David's six,

00:57:09.710 --> 00:57:12.610
Amartya Sen actually had
quite a well-known paper.

00:57:12.610 --> 00:57:15.640
I believe it was in the
Journal of Political Economy

00:57:15.640 --> 00:57:17.470
where he made precisely this,

00:57:17.470 --> 00:57:21.713
where he formalized this
point precisely that actually,

00:57:21.713 --> 00:57:23.510
it might look to you

00:57:23.510 --> 00:57:25.910
as though there's
unlimited supply of labor

00:57:25.910 --> 00:57:27.570
but actually other
members of the household

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:29.280
are adjusting their labor supply

00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:33.360
to keep everything as it is.

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:35.580
And guess who's actually
adjusting their labor supply

00:57:35.580 --> 00:57:39.870
when a man leaves for the city?

00:57:39.870 --> 00:57:41.486
- Thank you Ravi.

00:57:41.486 --> 00:57:42.319
Gerry, anything you wanna add on that

00:57:42.319 --> 00:57:43.433
in terms of Arthur Lewis?

00:57:44.417 --> 00:57:46.110
- Well in terms of Arthur Lewis,

00:57:46.110 --> 00:57:47.580
the one thing I would say

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:50.730
is that when he was still in his 40s,

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:55.030
when he was dealing with
Ghana and Kwame Nkrumah,

00:57:55.030 --> 00:57:57.970
and that one of the characteristics

00:57:57.970 --> 00:58:00.660
of Arthur Lewis that I discovered

00:58:00.660 --> 00:58:02.940
was that even on important questions,

00:58:02.940 --> 00:58:06.620
he was willing, at least later in his life

00:58:06.620 --> 00:58:08.540
to change his mind.

00:58:08.540 --> 00:58:12.510
And I think that in some
conversations I had with him

00:58:12.510 --> 00:58:14.160
but even more importantly,

00:58:14.160 --> 00:58:17.840
in some formal lectures
that I heard him give,

00:58:17.840 --> 00:58:21.210
he had mellowed a bit
from what we might call

00:58:21.210 --> 00:58:23.760
a younger man in his 40s,

00:58:23.760 --> 00:58:28.640
sort of hardcore, technical,
this is the economist

00:58:28.640 --> 00:58:31.287
and these politics shouldn't
be getting in the way.

00:58:33.716 --> 00:58:34.700
- That kind of gets back to something Ravi

00:58:34.700 --> 00:58:35.560
was mentioning earlier

00:58:35.560 --> 00:58:38.660
in terms of the evolution of his thinking.

00:58:38.660 --> 00:58:41.510
So I appreciate that we
were able to go into that

00:58:41.510 --> 00:58:42.860
in a little bit more depth.

00:58:43.720 --> 00:58:45.410
We have some other great
questions coming in,

00:58:45.410 --> 00:58:46.243
keep them coming.

00:58:46.243 --> 00:58:51.243
Now some of them are
bringing up other you know,

00:58:51.573 --> 00:58:53.790
early thinkers in development economics

00:58:53.790 --> 00:58:56.040
who perhaps should also be
part of this conversation.

00:58:56.040 --> 00:58:57.020
People from around the world

00:58:57.020 --> 00:59:00.510
who've contributed to
the field and you know,

00:59:00.510 --> 00:59:02.060
I guess it points to the importance

00:59:02.060 --> 00:59:03.350
of having a conversation like this

00:59:03.350 --> 00:59:05.510
because we don't talk about
these individuals enough

00:59:05.510 --> 00:59:08.070
but again, just trying
to build a connection

00:59:08.070 --> 00:59:13.070
between the people you all are
focused on in your research.

00:59:13.240 --> 00:59:16.500
This is one of the questions
posed in the description

00:59:16.500 --> 00:59:17.333
of this webinar.

00:59:17.333 --> 00:59:19.690
We talked about what set the stage

00:59:19.690 --> 00:59:22.807
for this pioneering group of
thinkers to define development.

00:59:22.807 --> 00:59:23.750
And I guess I will ask that.

00:59:23.750 --> 00:59:27.410
What, but I wanna broaden
that a little bit.

00:59:27.410 --> 00:59:30.630
So what was the context
that allowed these thinkers

00:59:30.630 --> 00:59:32.870
to make an impact on
the field of development

00:59:32.870 --> 00:59:34.960
and what were some of
the very real challenges

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:37.730
that stood in the way of
the being able to make

00:59:37.730 --> 00:59:38.779
the impact they could have?

00:59:38.779 --> 00:59:41.790
So we've talked about people
who have been recognized

00:59:41.790 --> 00:59:44.920
and received awards for really,

00:59:44.920 --> 00:59:46.460
their contributions to the field

00:59:46.460 --> 00:59:48.560
but also people who maybe
didn't get the recognition

00:59:48.560 --> 00:59:50.630
they should have and
there are many other names

00:59:50.630 --> 00:59:52.330
who aren't a part of this webinar.

00:59:53.589 --> 00:59:54.422
So I just wonder if any of you

00:59:54.422 --> 00:59:55.440
can talk a little bit more about,

00:59:55.440 --> 00:59:58.260
not just the people but
the context around them.

00:59:58.260 --> 01:00:03.190
Including issues like
racism, sexism that you know,

01:00:04.070 --> 01:00:06.410
what were the conditions that set them up

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:07.690
to make a difference in the field

01:00:07.690 --> 01:00:10.163
and what were some of the
constraints that held them back?

01:00:12.790 --> 01:00:13.880
David?
- I would say

01:00:13.880 --> 01:00:16.850
that the overriding precondition for it

01:00:16.850 --> 01:00:19.600
is the partial and uneven decolonization

01:00:20.502 --> 01:00:23.840
which provided new forms of independence,

01:00:23.840 --> 01:00:27.440
especially the political independence

01:00:27.440 --> 01:00:31.830
but also created reconstructed
and reconfigured forms

01:00:31.830 --> 01:00:33.760
of economic dependence.

01:00:33.760 --> 01:00:36.240
That admitted people
born colonial subjects

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:39.630
into the highest citadels
of Western learning,

01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:42.603
in the 1950s especially in the U.S..

01:00:42.603 --> 01:00:44.730
Excuse me, in the United
Kingdom by the 1960s,

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:46.610
increasingly in the United States

01:00:46.610 --> 01:00:50.815
but also presented them with constraints

01:00:50.815 --> 01:00:52.980
and uneven treatment.

01:00:52.980 --> 01:00:55.860
I actually asked a number of people who,

01:00:55.860 --> 01:00:58.940
well of these six as well as
people who studied with them

01:00:58.940 --> 01:01:01.470
in the 1950s who were from South Asia

01:01:01.470 --> 01:01:04.150
and almost to a person they denied

01:01:04.150 --> 01:01:08.170
that they were treated any
differently in this time

01:01:08.170 --> 01:01:11.120
but you also get a counter narrative.

01:01:11.120 --> 01:01:13.720
The widow of one of them told me that

01:01:13.720 --> 01:01:15.320
Amartya Sen and Lal Jayawardena

01:01:15.320 --> 01:01:17.120
used to get confused all the time.

01:01:17.120 --> 01:01:19.130
Like people would
confuse one for the other

01:01:19.130 --> 01:01:21.830
even though they look
remarkably different.

01:01:21.830 --> 01:01:25.610
Amartya Sen is sort of
tall, rail thin angular face

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:27.940
and Lal Jayawardena, completely different

01:01:27.940 --> 01:01:31.840
and yet they were constantly confused.

01:01:31.840 --> 01:01:35.850
Amartya Sen even told me that
he faced no discrimination.

01:01:35.850 --> 01:01:38.140
He said, "Either for or against"

01:01:38.140 --> 01:01:40.437
on the basis of his South Asian descent

01:01:42.279 --> 01:01:45.080
and so this unevenness I
think is really important.

01:01:45.080 --> 01:01:47.640
It removes some important inequalities

01:01:47.640 --> 01:01:52.640
but left in place or even
reinforced other kinds of,

01:01:53.424 --> 01:01:55.860
at the kind of structural level

01:01:55.860 --> 01:01:58.150
about forms of inequality.

01:01:58.150 --> 01:02:02.560
So I would say that the
incompleteness of decolonization.

01:02:02.560 --> 01:02:07.440
It was a long process, it was
an incredibly uneven process

01:02:07.440 --> 01:02:10.883
that all of these people
are entering into.

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:12.833
- Thanks David.

01:02:12.833 --> 01:02:13.940
Amna, anything to add

01:02:13.940 --> 01:02:16.510
in terms of Akhtar Hameed Khan

01:02:16.510 --> 01:02:20.520
and the constraints, the context?

01:02:20.520 --> 01:02:22.422
Both opportunities and constraints

01:02:22.422 --> 01:02:25.823
in terms of his ability
to really leave a legacy?

01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:29.552
- Yes I actually think David,

01:02:29.552 --> 01:02:31.510
uneven decolonization is such a,

01:02:31.510 --> 01:02:35.570
it's such a good way
to think about the fear

01:02:35.570 --> 01:02:36.953
in the 50s and the 60s

01:02:36.953 --> 01:02:39.680
that we're all I think sort
of collectively thinking about

01:02:39.680 --> 01:02:43.120
because while some
hierarchies, global hierarchies

01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:46.760
might have been reconceptualized,

01:02:46.760 --> 01:02:48.920
new hierarchies were created.

01:02:48.920 --> 01:02:51.030
Even within the
postcolonial nation states.

01:02:51.030 --> 01:02:55.810
So I think, so for Akhtar Hameed Khan

01:02:55.810 --> 01:02:56.860
I think the goals were,

01:02:56.860 --> 01:02:59.433
geopolitical context
is extremely important.

01:03:00.280 --> 01:03:02.850
So while older forms
of colonial knowledge,

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:05.470
about development were still in place

01:03:05.470 --> 01:03:06.970
but there were these
new forms of knowledge

01:03:06.970 --> 01:03:09.275
that were also being created.

01:03:09.275 --> 01:03:11.930
So if we look at Pakistan as an example,

01:03:11.930 --> 01:03:16.930
I think we can't discount the co-creation

01:03:16.930 --> 01:03:19.900
of academic knowledge in
so many different fields

01:03:19.900 --> 01:03:22.990
which was at many times funded by,

01:03:22.990 --> 01:03:26.300
as even Rohini mentioned
in the introduction,

01:03:26.300 --> 01:03:29.780
the Ford Foundation and
a particular institute

01:03:29.780 --> 01:03:32.063
such as the Economic Growth Center.

01:03:33.050 --> 01:03:35.820
So for Akhtar Hameed Khan,

01:03:35.820 --> 01:03:40.170
his trajectory in the 1960s was,

01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:44.580
he kept bumping up against Ayub
Khan's authoritarian regime

01:03:44.580 --> 01:03:47.695
and by the sort of,

01:03:47.695 --> 01:03:51.100
the traditional account is in 1971

01:03:51.100 --> 01:03:53.769
he's sort of forced to flee Comilla

01:03:53.769 --> 01:03:55.560
because of the Bangladesh
war of liberation

01:03:55.560 --> 01:03:59.630
but since the late 1960s
you can sort of see

01:03:59.630 --> 01:04:01.310
that the state is,

01:04:01.310 --> 01:04:05.350
Ayub Khan's state is
getting more insistent

01:04:05.350 --> 01:04:08.900
on putting forward more status programs

01:04:08.900 --> 01:04:11.640
of development in Comilla
where Akhtar Hameed Khan

01:04:11.640 --> 01:04:15.763
then regularly had
disagreements and you know,

01:04:16.827 --> 01:04:18.630
threatens to resign, he
does resign for awhile,

01:04:18.630 --> 01:04:20.353
then he's sort of reinstated.

01:04:21.485 --> 01:04:26.200
So I think, but then he is also backed

01:04:26.200 --> 01:04:29.040
by the Ford Foundation advisors.

01:04:29.040 --> 01:04:32.430
So there is all these Cold
War geopolitical tensions

01:04:32.430 --> 01:04:35.860
that really, and the alignments

01:04:35.860 --> 01:04:38.404
with an authoritarian
regime that really sort of

01:04:38.404 --> 01:04:43.404
make this project really
messy and once you know,

01:04:45.760 --> 01:04:47.343
Bangladesh becomes independent,

01:04:48.230 --> 01:04:52.080
then whatever the legacy of Comilla was

01:04:52.080 --> 01:04:54.740
is then reimagined in new ways.

01:04:54.740 --> 01:04:58.490
And it does inform in the 1980s

01:04:58.490 --> 01:05:00.270
some of the micro development approaches

01:05:00.270 --> 01:05:03.250
that are then pioneered in Bangladesh

01:05:03.250 --> 01:05:07.390
but it's a story of where politics

01:05:08.492 --> 01:05:10.970
frequently rubs up against

01:05:10.970 --> 01:05:14.443
what the development economics
practitioners have in mind.

01:05:15.660 --> 01:05:17.185
- Thank you Amna.

01:05:17.185 --> 01:05:18.380
I wanna bring in another
question from the audience.

01:05:18.380 --> 01:05:22.210
There are a few questions
around early thinkers

01:05:22.210 --> 01:05:25.479
in development economics
from Latin America

01:05:25.479 --> 01:05:29.520
and that might not
necessarily be the focus

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:31.120
in terms of our panelists' expertise.

01:05:31.120 --> 01:05:34.240
But just questions around
these early thinkers

01:05:34.240 --> 01:05:35.073
from Latin America

01:05:35.073 --> 01:05:38.600
perhaps being missing from this discussion

01:05:38.600 --> 01:05:40.810
and I think one of the
interesting points that came up

01:05:40.810 --> 01:05:44.190
is how much of this work
was originally in Spanish

01:05:44.190 --> 01:05:46.550
and sometimes lost in
translation to English

01:05:46.550 --> 01:05:49.270
which is perhaps a challenge globally

01:05:49.270 --> 01:05:52.192
when we talk about great thinking

01:05:52.192 --> 01:05:54.640
in development economics
happening from different countries

01:05:54.640 --> 01:05:56.280
where English is not the primary language.

01:05:56.280 --> 01:05:59.050
So I just wanted to kind
of throw that out there.

01:05:59.050 --> 01:06:01.060
Whether you wanna talk about
Latin America specifically

01:06:01.060 --> 01:06:03.440
or this challenge of
language in translation.

01:06:03.440 --> 01:06:05.630
I just think it's an
important point to bring up.

01:06:05.630 --> 01:06:09.437
Any reactions from our panelists, David?

01:06:09.437 --> 01:06:12.560
- I mean I agree with the
thrust of the critique,

01:06:12.560 --> 01:06:15.270
that we're leaving out Latin America here.

01:06:15.270 --> 01:06:19.450
I think in some parts
this is a conceptual sense

01:06:19.450 --> 01:06:21.470
of the time that the kind of,

01:06:21.470 --> 01:06:22.840
the action if you will,

01:06:22.840 --> 01:06:27.734
was in newly, newly independent nations.

01:06:27.734 --> 01:06:30.020
So the Caribbean gained independence

01:06:30.020 --> 01:06:32.640
where Lewis and others are from.

01:06:32.640 --> 01:06:37.640
Only in the 1960s but most
of the rest of continental

01:06:38.192 --> 01:06:42.000
Central and South America
were already independent.

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:44.980
So decolonization as a name,
it sort of leaves out this.

01:06:45.858 --> 01:06:47.280
But I think the work that Raul Prebisch

01:06:47.280 --> 01:06:49.680
and many others, many, many others did

01:06:49.680 --> 01:06:53.030
at the Economic Commission
for Latin America is crucial.

01:06:53.030 --> 01:06:55.470
What's striking to me and
I've often found this puzzling

01:06:55.470 --> 01:06:59.640
is how little the South Asian economists

01:06:59.640 --> 01:07:02.090
connected with Latin Americans.

01:07:02.090 --> 01:07:06.640
Either personally or engaging
in, or even just in print.

01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:09.130
That there's this really strong disconnect

01:07:09.130 --> 01:07:11.970
in the 50s and well into the 1960s

01:07:11.970 --> 01:07:15.420
between these two types
of brilliant economists

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:16.330
in both sides

01:07:16.330 --> 01:07:20.193
working independently on
many of the same questions.

01:07:21.030 --> 01:07:22.680
And I think one of the
questions we might ask

01:07:22.680 --> 01:07:25.028
is what's responsible for that,

01:07:25.028 --> 01:07:30.028
for that barrier or missed
connection if you will?

01:07:31.050 --> 01:07:33.460
- Yeah great point, I'm
glad you brought that up.

01:07:33.460 --> 01:07:35.560
Ravi, did you wanna jump in?

01:07:35.560 --> 01:07:39.140
- Yeah, just a quick,
a little nugget of an.

01:07:39.140 --> 01:07:41.160
Just an observation.

01:07:41.160 --> 01:07:46.160
Arthur Lewis, after he left Ghana went to,

01:07:46.610 --> 01:07:49.550
only came back once and that was in 1963

01:07:49.550 --> 01:07:52.480
for a conference on the seven-year plan.

01:07:52.480 --> 01:07:55.790
Ghana's seven-year plan and
here are some of the people

01:07:55.790 --> 01:07:58.470
who were actually
present at the discussion

01:07:58.470 --> 01:08:00.840
of that seven-year plan.

01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:05.060
It's Albert Hirschman, Nicholas Kaldor,

01:08:05.060 --> 01:08:08.210
Osvaldo Sunkel and K. N. Raj.

01:08:08.210 --> 01:08:11.340
The person I mentioned earlier

01:08:11.340 --> 01:08:12.810
as being very, very important.

01:08:12.810 --> 01:08:14.700
So you know, the point is I think,

01:08:14.700 --> 01:08:19.389
the occasions for these
people to meet and discuss

01:08:19.389 --> 01:08:21.340
were few and far between.

01:08:21.340 --> 01:08:23.360
Because we did this in the 1960s and so on

01:08:23.360 --> 01:08:25.650
and travel and so on and so forth

01:08:25.650 --> 01:08:28.510
and the creation of these UN,

01:08:28.510 --> 01:08:32.510
the Economic Commission
for Asia and the Far East

01:08:32.510 --> 01:08:33.960
which was ECAFE at the time,

01:08:33.960 --> 01:08:36.100
it's now ESCAP, Economic
and Social Commission

01:08:36.100 --> 01:08:37.230
for Asia and the Pacific

01:08:37.230 --> 01:08:40.080
and then ECLAC in Latin
America, et cetera.

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:45.080
In some sense, I suppose segmented
the discourse in that way

01:08:46.430 --> 01:08:49.753
and it's not difficult to
imagine that it was difficult

01:08:49.753 --> 01:08:53.000
for actual get togethers in that time.

01:08:53.000 --> 01:08:54.450
But here's one small example

01:08:54.450 --> 01:08:57.973
where they all came to assess Ghana's,

01:08:58.920 --> 01:09:03.920
and Ghana's plan from Latin
America, from India and so on.

01:09:05.703 --> 01:09:06.770
- Very interesting and kind of echoing

01:09:06.770 --> 01:09:08.570
your point earlier,

01:09:08.570 --> 01:09:11.870
when there was this question
about academic journals.

01:09:11.870 --> 01:09:15.620
At least a point of
optimism is sharing work

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:17.979
across geographies is easier today

01:09:17.979 --> 01:09:19.070
than it was at that time.

01:09:19.070 --> 01:09:22.770
For scholars to be able to
come together despite distance.

01:09:22.770 --> 01:09:26.270
I wanna get to this great
question from the audience

01:09:26.270 --> 01:09:28.070
and it points to something
that I was hoping

01:09:28.070 --> 01:09:29.810
to get to in our conversation.

01:09:29.810 --> 01:09:31.160
One of the things I
wanted to really make sure

01:09:31.160 --> 01:09:33.440
we talk about is, you know,

01:09:33.440 --> 01:09:36.050
how are these figures
that we've talked about,

01:09:36.050 --> 01:09:37.020
from history,

01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:39.540
continuing to inform
development research, policy

01:09:39.540 --> 01:09:40.917
and practice

01:09:40.917 --> 01:09:42.760
and some of you alluded
to that in your remarks.

01:09:42.760 --> 01:09:47.090
But here's a specific question
on their legacy today.

01:09:47.090 --> 01:09:49.040
So this comes from the audience.

01:09:49.040 --> 01:09:52.260
Thinking about the turn
towards poverty reduction

01:09:52.260 --> 01:09:54.470
and away from an earlier era of focus

01:09:54.470 --> 01:09:56.810
on addressing global inequality,

01:09:56.810 --> 01:09:59.650
how did these economists
relate to this turn?

01:09:59.650 --> 01:10:01.150
What role did they play in it?

01:10:05.719 --> 01:10:06.552
David?

01:10:06.552 --> 01:10:07.385
- I can start with it.

01:10:07.385 --> 01:10:11.020
I mean at an institutional level,

01:10:11.020 --> 01:10:13.810
the World Bank, then
the and I suppose now,

01:10:13.810 --> 01:10:17.050
probably still the
largest single institution

01:10:17.050 --> 01:10:19.333
in the development landscape.

01:10:20.450 --> 01:10:22.770
Their turn toward poverty eradication

01:10:22.770 --> 01:10:26.760
came primarily through the
efforts of Mahbub ul Haq

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:31.320
who convinced a dynamic,
new World Bank President,

01:10:31.320 --> 01:10:35.350
perhaps doing penance for
his leaving the Vietnam War

01:10:35.350 --> 01:10:39.950
for the 1960s, Robert McNamara to push,

01:10:39.950 --> 01:10:43.200
to change the focus at
an institutional level

01:10:43.200 --> 01:10:47.800
of the world towards poverty reduction.

01:10:47.800 --> 01:10:51.451
But I then, I also think that
sticking with Haq for a second

01:10:51.451 --> 01:10:55.250
the focus on human development
helps us really understand

01:10:55.250 --> 01:10:58.040
the rise of NGOs on the one hand

01:10:58.040 --> 01:11:01.300
which were well-equipped
to handle questions

01:11:01.300 --> 01:11:03.310
like health and education

01:11:03.310 --> 01:11:06.730
which were kind of factored
into human development

01:11:06.730 --> 01:11:09.300
beyond just national income.

01:11:09.300 --> 01:11:10.840
And so we can see in this,

01:11:10.840 --> 01:11:15.840
I wouldn't say that Haq
predicted or made this happen

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:20.200
but it was consonant with a move away from

01:11:20.200 --> 01:11:22.260
big national institutions,

01:11:22.260 --> 01:11:26.450
toward international NGOs and even local,

01:11:26.450 --> 01:11:27.923
regional and local NGOs.

01:11:29.070 --> 01:11:31.090
Taking on smaller scale,

01:11:31.090 --> 01:11:32.590
taking on smaller scale projects

01:11:32.590 --> 01:11:35.730
rather than just advising
national governments

01:11:35.730 --> 01:11:37.783
on their national plan.

01:11:38.730 --> 01:11:41.440
- Really interesting, that's
a world I cover closely

01:11:41.440 --> 01:11:44.400
and honestly I was not as
familiar with his influence

01:11:44.400 --> 01:11:48.140
on the development of,
really the whole NGO sector.

01:11:48.140 --> 01:11:49.800
So really interesting.

01:11:49.800 --> 01:11:54.260
Other thoughts in terms of
more broadly how you know,

01:11:54.260 --> 01:11:55.980
these figures that we focused on today

01:11:55.980 --> 01:11:59.110
continue to affect development economics,

01:11:59.110 --> 01:12:02.830
development policy and this
turn away from addressing

01:12:02.830 --> 01:12:05.210
global inequality towards
poverty reduction.

01:12:05.210 --> 01:12:07.730
Which perhaps some might
argue may turn again

01:12:07.730 --> 01:12:09.580
toward addressing global inequality

01:12:09.580 --> 01:12:11.480
given some of the issues we're seeing.

01:12:12.570 --> 01:12:14.847
Gerry, did you wanna jump in on that?

01:12:14.847 --> 01:12:17.523
- Yeah but just a short, small point.

01:12:19.148 --> 01:12:23.810
Let's not forget that reducing poverty

01:12:23.810 --> 01:12:28.500
is reducing global inequality and in fact,

01:12:28.500 --> 01:12:32.030
if one were thinking about
what is the quickest way

01:12:32.030 --> 01:12:35.860
in the short run that
I could try to reduce

01:12:35.860 --> 01:12:40.210
global inequality would be
to try to reduce poverty.

01:12:40.210 --> 01:12:43.333
One way of thinking about this is that,

01:12:44.500 --> 01:12:46.440
'cause there was also an earlier question

01:12:46.440 --> 01:12:48.860
about the rain is fay,

01:12:48.860 --> 01:12:53.753
more a formalization of Lewis' model

01:12:55.221 --> 01:12:57.040
which ignored various issues

01:12:57.040 --> 01:13:00.800
having to do with inequality
and things like that.

01:13:00.800 --> 01:13:02.360
One way of thinking about that

01:13:02.360 --> 01:13:05.520
is those are models and ways of thinking

01:13:05.520 --> 01:13:09.860
that are looking at policies and issues

01:13:09.860 --> 01:13:11.763
and their long run effects.

01:13:12.860 --> 01:13:16.910
As opposed to thinking
about the short term effects

01:13:16.910 --> 01:13:21.534
of policies and trying to do
something in the short term.

01:13:21.534 --> 01:13:26.330
And I think that this turn
that's being talked about

01:13:26.330 --> 01:13:27.980
towards poverty reduction

01:13:27.980 --> 01:13:31.130
which is kind of getting
at the more micro level

01:13:31.130 --> 01:13:33.240
of where individual people are

01:13:33.240 --> 01:13:36.523
or individual communities
are are in effect,

01:13:36.523 --> 01:13:38.650
thinking about I wanna do
something about poverty

01:13:38.650 --> 01:13:43.070
in the next five years or I
wanna change global inequality

01:13:43.070 --> 01:13:46.300
even though it may not be some huge change

01:13:46.300 --> 01:13:48.222
in the short run.

01:13:48.222 --> 01:13:50.060
So I think we have to put those questions

01:13:50.060 --> 01:13:52.223
into some kind of perspective.

01:13:53.430 --> 01:13:56.639
- Thanks Gerry, other thoughts on this?

01:13:56.639 --> 01:13:58.980
Otherwise a couple more
questions rolling in, Ravi.

01:13:58.980 --> 01:14:03.000
- Yeah I suppose just
reacting a little bit

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:04.463
to Gerry's point.

01:14:05.380 --> 01:14:07.870
I suppose the concern might be that the,

01:14:07.870 --> 01:14:10.820
what if the causality runs
from global inequality

01:14:10.820 --> 01:14:13.729
to poverty in individual countries.

01:14:13.729 --> 01:14:14.680
I suppose that's the nature of the things.

01:14:14.680 --> 01:14:16.210
So I suppose in an accounting sense

01:14:16.210 --> 01:14:19.221
if I reduce poverty in a country,

01:14:19.221 --> 01:14:20.054
than almost by definition,

01:14:20.054 --> 01:14:22.210
global inequality as measured will reduce.

01:14:22.210 --> 01:14:25.199
But does the global
inequality in some sense,

01:14:25.199 --> 01:14:29.070
does it have the seeds of
the structural processes

01:14:29.070 --> 01:14:32.357
which lead to poverty in these countries.

01:14:32.357 --> 01:14:33.749
Now that will be,

01:14:33.749 --> 01:14:35.660
now you know Arthur Lewis again,
going back to Arthur Lewis,

01:14:35.660 --> 01:14:40.530
he had some, his surplus labor theory

01:14:40.530 --> 01:14:43.680
actually also helped
him understand he says,

01:14:43.680 --> 01:14:45.330
global inequalities.

01:14:45.330 --> 01:14:47.597
So he has this phrase where he says,

01:14:47.597 --> 01:14:50.047
"When I look at plantations," he says.

01:14:50.047 --> 01:14:52.257
"There's been tremendous
technical progress

01:14:52.257 --> 01:14:57.027
"in efficiency gains in the
production of plantation crops

01:14:57.027 --> 01:14:58.959
"over the last 100 years."

01:14:58.959 --> 01:14:59.983
Or whatever.

01:14:59.983 --> 01:15:01.851
"And yet," he said, I remember the phrase.

01:15:01.851 --> 01:15:06.343
He says, "Plantation workers
are bare feet and wear rags."

01:15:07.254 --> 01:15:08.769
Something like that he said.

01:15:08.769 --> 01:15:09.680
A question is why.

01:15:09.680 --> 01:15:11.840
It's because their wages are held down

01:15:11.840 --> 01:15:15.250
by this unlimited supply
of labor that's coming in

01:15:15.250 --> 01:15:16.490
from the countryside.

01:15:16.490 --> 01:15:18.560
So all the gains of technical progress

01:15:18.560 --> 01:15:22.320
are actually going to the
owners of the plantation,

01:15:22.320 --> 01:15:25.239
not to the workers.

01:15:25.239 --> 01:15:28.940
It's when that surplus
labor ends, phase ends

01:15:28.940 --> 01:15:31.920
and wages start rising,
labor markets tighten,

01:15:31.920 --> 01:15:33.880
that's when you start
to get the effect on,

01:15:33.880 --> 01:15:37.660
good effects on inequality
of the technical change.

01:15:37.660 --> 01:15:39.510
And of course, if one looks
at China's development

01:15:39.510 --> 01:15:42.070
just now to bring it to
a contemporary frame,

01:15:42.070 --> 01:15:44.580
that is one, that would be one argument

01:15:44.580 --> 01:15:47.890
as to why it is that
inequality rose so sharply

01:15:47.890 --> 01:15:50.400
in the Chinese context.

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:52.810
Because there's this massive
supply of so to speak,

01:15:52.810 --> 01:15:55.700
surplus labor coming in from rural areas.

01:15:55.700 --> 01:15:57.780
Essentially holding wages lower

01:15:57.780 --> 01:15:59.650
than they otherwise would have been

01:15:59.650 --> 01:16:02.150
and it was only really when

01:16:02.150 --> 01:16:04.730
the so-called Lewis
turning point was reached,

01:16:04.730 --> 01:16:07.690
when the labor markets started
tightening in rural areas,

01:16:07.690 --> 01:16:09.830
that wages started rising in rural areas,

01:16:09.830 --> 01:16:13.040
that maybe some positive effects

01:16:13.040 --> 01:16:16.329
on the reduction of inequality
started coming through.

01:16:16.329 --> 01:16:17.177
So that if you like,

01:16:17.177 --> 01:16:19.675
is a translation of the Lewisian
perspective from the 1950s

01:16:19.675 --> 01:16:24.470
to the current Chinese discourse.

01:16:24.470 --> 01:16:27.940
Some of us have been arguing
that maybe Chinese inequality,

01:16:27.940 --> 01:16:31.400
at least is not increasing
at the rate that it was

01:16:32.405 --> 01:16:34.744
and one of the arguments is that,

01:16:34.744 --> 01:16:38.773
that the Lewis turning point
so to speak has been reached.

01:16:40.323 --> 01:16:42.740
- So I wanna give enough time and space

01:16:42.740 --> 01:16:45.400
to throw out one final question

01:16:45.400 --> 01:16:48.260
and given the complexity
of what we're discussing,

01:16:48.260 --> 01:16:49.860
I wanna make sure that
each of our speakers

01:16:49.860 --> 01:16:52.623
have really a couple minutes
for any final thoughts.

01:16:53.483 --> 01:16:54.316
But first I wanna bring in

01:16:54.316 --> 01:16:56.546
what's a comment from the audience

01:16:56.546 --> 01:16:57.860
that I think is highly relevant.

01:16:57.860 --> 01:16:59.290
The comment is,

01:16:59.290 --> 01:17:02.670
it would be great if the
idea of decolonization

01:17:02.670 --> 01:17:05.740
transcended the list of early greats

01:17:05.740 --> 01:17:07.060
in development economics

01:17:07.060 --> 01:17:09.320
to encompass the new
understandings of the term

01:17:09.320 --> 01:17:10.860
that have emerged among people working

01:17:10.860 --> 01:17:12.910
in the Global South today.

01:17:12.910 --> 01:17:16.120
So I think really that comment

01:17:16.120 --> 01:17:19.270
is connecting this
conversation we're having

01:17:19.270 --> 01:17:20.610
in the opening session,

01:17:20.610 --> 01:17:23.440
talking about these early thinkers

01:17:23.440 --> 01:17:25.800
who influenced the field
of development economics

01:17:25.800 --> 01:17:27.920
to the themes of this symposium

01:17:27.920 --> 01:17:31.290
that will continue Friday October 29th

01:17:31.290 --> 01:17:33.527
as well as Friday November 5th.

01:17:33.527 --> 01:17:35.959
So again the point is,

01:17:35.959 --> 01:17:39.570
how do we talk not only
about these early greats

01:17:39.570 --> 01:17:41.050
and the legacy they left,

01:17:41.050 --> 01:17:43.030
but new understandings of the term

01:17:43.030 --> 01:17:46.490
of the decolonization of
development economics?

01:17:46.490 --> 01:17:48.670
Especially for people working

01:17:48.670 --> 01:17:50.580
in low and middle income countries today

01:17:50.580 --> 01:17:53.250
and so to turn that into
the form of a question

01:17:53.250 --> 01:17:56.140
and really give each of
you a little bit of time

01:17:56.140 --> 01:17:58.550
to connect the people you talked about

01:17:58.550 --> 01:18:00.610
with the themes of this symposium.

01:18:00.610 --> 01:18:03.610
Do you have any final
thoughts on the importance

01:18:03.610 --> 01:18:05.310
of considering these early voices.

01:18:05.310 --> 01:18:07.900
As we gear up for the
symposium focused on race,

01:18:07.900 --> 01:18:11.900
ethnicity, gender, economic
justice and global development.

01:18:11.900 --> 01:18:15.340
And we'll start with David, Ravi and Amna

01:18:15.340 --> 01:18:17.730
and then I'd love to close
with Rohini and Gerry

01:18:17.730 --> 01:18:20.550
and help connect this opening session

01:18:20.550 --> 01:18:21.780
to the larger symposium.

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:23.230
David, can we start with you?

01:18:24.778 --> 01:18:26.410
- I was afraid you were
gonna say that Catherine.

01:18:26.410 --> 01:18:28.020
It's a really big question.
- It is.

01:18:28.020 --> 01:18:29.144
- I'm not exactly sure,

01:18:29.144 --> 01:18:31.380
exactly sure how to take it.

01:18:31.380 --> 01:18:35.157
I mean I do think that
that question of inequality

01:18:37.930 --> 01:18:40.438
that was so important to these figures

01:18:40.438 --> 01:18:45.438
really remains a central
question, a question today.

01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:48.410
And it was there, that
was the right question

01:18:48.410 --> 01:18:49.510
that they were asking.

01:18:50.427 --> 01:18:51.260
I might actually add to it

01:18:51.260 --> 01:18:52.560
something that's come a little bit,

01:18:52.560 --> 01:18:53.393
I guess a weak theme,

01:18:53.393 --> 01:18:55.890
particularly in some of Ravi's comments

01:18:55.890 --> 01:18:58.140
which is a kind of temporal inequality.

01:18:58.140 --> 01:19:00.040
One of the things that those scholars

01:19:00.040 --> 01:19:02.630
I'm writing about were focused on

01:19:02.630 --> 01:19:05.620
was consumption now versus
consumption later right?

01:19:05.620 --> 01:19:08.890
And the idea would be
that some sacrifices now

01:19:08.890 --> 01:19:12.020
will provides goods later.

01:19:12.020 --> 01:19:14.350
Joan Robinson perhaps typically

01:19:14.350 --> 01:19:18.238
had a great sentence about
that when she visited Sri Lanka

01:19:18.238 --> 01:19:20.770
in the late 1950s and saw
this robust welfare state,

01:19:20.770 --> 01:19:21.933
social welfare state.

01:19:22.870 --> 01:19:24.347
That she said that,

01:19:24.347 --> 01:19:27.997
"Sri Lanka was eating the
fruit before growing the tree."

01:19:29.304 --> 01:19:32.400
That it was spending rather than saving

01:19:32.400 --> 01:19:34.810
for the short term and so
I think one of the issues

01:19:34.810 --> 01:19:37.330
that we see people like
Amartya Sen deal with

01:19:37.330 --> 01:19:39.810
in academics and formal language

01:19:39.810 --> 01:19:42.270
and we see others like
Mahbub ul Haq deal with

01:19:42.270 --> 01:19:44.350
in a kind of more political vein is

01:19:44.350 --> 01:19:46.050
this other dimension of inequality

01:19:46.050 --> 01:19:50.650
which is how do you balance
the current against the future?

01:19:50.650 --> 01:19:52.050
And it's plausible to think

01:19:52.050 --> 01:19:57.010
that with our current
interest in global inequality,

01:19:57.010 --> 01:20:01.600
certainly since 2008, even
just in economic terms,

01:20:01.600 --> 01:20:03.360
that that becomes central.

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:05.820
I would say that this group,

01:20:05.820 --> 01:20:07.524
for all of their insights,

01:20:07.524 --> 01:20:08.357
the ones that I'm looking at,

01:20:08.357 --> 01:20:10.660
were not necessarily the
right ones to look at

01:20:10.660 --> 01:20:15.444
the interface of the structures
of economic inequality

01:20:15.444 --> 01:20:18.598
with other structures of inequality.

01:20:18.598 --> 01:20:21.670
And it's too kind of a vexing question

01:20:21.670 --> 01:20:24.180
to answer directly right now

01:20:24.180 --> 01:20:26.540
but I would say that
when you ask economists

01:20:26.540 --> 01:20:28.660
to talk about inequality
it's not a surprise

01:20:28.660 --> 01:20:31.712
that they turn to economic inequality.

01:20:31.712 --> 01:20:32.611
- Yeah.
- And so perhaps

01:20:32.611 --> 01:20:33.444
the message is one of Amna's,

01:20:33.444 --> 01:20:37.414
about bringing in people
working on boundary,

01:20:37.414 --> 01:20:40.700
on the boundaries or
economics and its neighbors

01:20:40.700 --> 01:20:44.840
as a way to continue this
process moving forward.

01:20:45.940 --> 01:20:47.610
- Thanks David.

01:20:47.610 --> 01:20:48.443
Amna, can I bring you in next?

01:20:50.036 --> 01:20:51.970
- Sure, actually David
that's a great segue for me.

01:20:51.970 --> 01:20:54.900
So one of the things I was
gonna say is that what was,

01:20:54.900 --> 01:20:57.350
I mean the Comilla model obviously has

01:20:57.350 --> 01:20:58.280
its critiques and problems

01:20:58.280 --> 01:21:00.850
but the one thing that it was doing well

01:21:00.850 --> 01:21:03.270
is that it was interdisciplinary
in nature right?

01:21:03.270 --> 01:21:07.294
So it wasn't just economists
who were producing knowledge

01:21:07.294 --> 01:21:08.830
but it was also
anthropologists, sociologists

01:21:08.830 --> 01:21:12.250
and political scientists
who were there and really,

01:21:12.250 --> 01:21:14.970
they were living in Comilla,

01:21:14.970 --> 01:21:16.730
they were going out into the communities

01:21:16.730 --> 01:21:17.990
and working with people

01:21:17.990 --> 01:21:19.970
and producing these forms of knowledge.

01:21:19.970 --> 01:21:24.600
So I think maybe focusing
a little bit more

01:21:24.600 --> 01:21:27.560
and taking more seriously
local knowledges and practices

01:21:27.560 --> 01:21:29.170
and then also broadening our visions

01:21:29.170 --> 01:21:31.640
of what the political economy is.

01:21:31.640 --> 01:21:34.900
To really bring reproductive practices,

01:21:34.900 --> 01:21:37.560
to bring the family into focus

01:21:37.560 --> 01:21:39.870
when we think about political economy

01:21:39.870 --> 01:21:42.522
and it's not just about you know,

01:21:42.522 --> 01:21:45.490
rational economic agents.

01:21:45.490 --> 01:21:49.880
I think that might be sort of ways of,

01:21:49.880 --> 01:21:52.200
you know, some things we
should be thinking about

01:21:52.200 --> 01:21:53.859
as we move forward with economic lessons

01:21:53.859 --> 01:21:55.109
and maybe one last point.

01:21:56.376 --> 01:21:58.350
Thinking again about a lot of these,

01:21:58.350 --> 01:21:59.183
about my projects

01:21:59.183 --> 01:22:02.250
and where the funding is coming from.

01:22:02.250 --> 01:22:05.070
Is to think more seriously about sort of,

01:22:05.070 --> 01:22:08.375
who, what are the power
structures in place?

01:22:08.375 --> 01:22:13.375
And who gets to benefit
the most from these forms

01:22:13.550 --> 01:22:15.060
of expertise and funding?

01:22:15.060 --> 01:22:18.960
So just being more sensitive
to funding source as well.

01:22:19.907 --> 01:22:21.777
- Great point, thanks Amna.

01:22:21.777 --> 01:22:22.610
Ravi.

01:22:24.106 --> 01:22:25.625
- Yeah so let me just say,

01:22:25.625 --> 01:22:27.190
I mean I agree with all the
things that have been said

01:22:27.190 --> 01:22:30.660
on this thing and just
speaking as an economist,

01:22:30.660 --> 01:22:32.380
as an economist.

01:22:32.380 --> 01:22:34.918
You know economics has a lot of strengths

01:22:34.918 --> 01:22:36.400
but also a lot of weaknesses

01:22:36.400 --> 01:22:38.870
and I say that perhaps
the greatest weakness

01:22:38.870 --> 01:22:41.764
is that economists don't
know the weakness of their,

01:22:41.764 --> 01:22:43.077
the weaknesses of their discipline.

01:22:43.077 --> 01:22:44.900
They focus a lot on; strengths

01:22:44.900 --> 01:22:46.210
and there are a lot of strengths.

01:22:46.210 --> 01:22:47.263
No question about it.

01:22:48.611 --> 01:22:49.444
So I would say that for example,

01:22:49.444 --> 01:22:52.970
that we don't engage with history.

01:22:52.970 --> 01:22:55.060
We don't engage with the
history of our own disciple

01:22:55.060 --> 01:22:57.720
let alone history more generally

01:22:57.720 --> 01:22:59.210
and that's what we've been saying.

01:22:59.210 --> 01:23:01.310
That there are real insights to be had

01:23:01.310 --> 01:23:05.225
by going back and seeing
what these greats,

01:23:05.225 --> 01:23:07.853
how they interacted with
the world and so on.

01:23:09.262 --> 01:23:10.700
And the second thing
that we tend not to do

01:23:10.700 --> 01:23:14.253
is to interact with other
disciplines too much.

01:23:15.580 --> 01:23:19.090
And I think what this discussion has,

01:23:19.090 --> 01:23:21.330
what Amna was saying and others.

01:23:21.330 --> 01:23:23.250
Really that that engagement

01:23:23.250 --> 01:23:28.209
I think is not only leads to
good policymaking and so on,

01:23:28.209 --> 01:23:29.500
but it also actually
leads to good economics

01:23:29.500 --> 01:23:32.030
and defined in its own terms.

01:23:32.030 --> 01:23:33.380
So let me leave it at that.

01:23:34.416 --> 01:23:36.000
- Agreed and that's part
of why I'm so grateful

01:23:36.000 --> 01:23:38.580
that Rohini and Gerry
have brought us together

01:23:38.580 --> 01:23:40.881
because it's addressing

01:23:40.881 --> 01:23:42.890
some of those problems
you just articulated.

01:23:42.890 --> 01:23:46.720
This discipline not engaging
with its own history enough

01:23:46.720 --> 01:23:48.900
and not engaging enough
with other disciplines

01:23:48.900 --> 01:23:50.250
and I think this,

01:23:50.250 --> 01:23:52.140
this opening session of this symposium

01:23:52.140 --> 01:23:53.840
and the whole symposium
will be really valuable

01:23:53.840 --> 01:23:55.493
from that perspective.

01:23:55.493 --> 01:23:56.350
So Rohini, Jerry.

01:23:56.350 --> 01:23:57.500
You've kind of kicked us off

01:23:57.500 --> 01:24:00.440
in terms of why this conversation, why now

01:24:00.440 --> 01:24:03.500
and we'd love just any final
reflections from the two of you

01:24:03.500 --> 01:24:05.590
and maybe connecting this conversation

01:24:05.590 --> 01:24:08.808
that we're having today
to the larger symposium.

01:24:08.808 --> 01:24:09.641
Gerry, you wanna start?

01:24:10.592 --> 01:24:12.510
- Okay, well just very briefly,

01:24:12.510 --> 01:24:16.325
I think that the last
comments that were made

01:24:16.325 --> 01:24:18.960
kind of would take us back to

01:24:18.960 --> 01:24:23.960
I think David was talking
about someone among his six

01:24:24.340 --> 01:24:27.890
who mentioned that at Cambridge,

01:24:27.890 --> 01:24:31.450
the economists back in the 40s or 50s

01:24:31.450 --> 01:24:34.810
weren't asking the right questions.

01:24:34.810 --> 01:24:37.610
Well at least they weren't
asking all the right questions.

01:24:37.610 --> 01:24:42.060
And that's where I think
the rest of the symposium

01:24:42.060 --> 01:24:44.071
is going to be trying to go.

01:24:44.071 --> 01:24:45.240
We've tried to bring together people

01:24:45.240 --> 01:24:47.830
who are asking questions that,

01:24:47.830 --> 01:24:49.300
that are somewhat different

01:24:49.300 --> 01:24:54.300
from what we're usually going
to see in economics journals

01:24:54.400 --> 01:24:58.000
and part of the reason
for that I would say,

01:24:58.000 --> 01:25:00.707
which connects to David's point

01:25:00.707 --> 01:25:03.770
and the one that Ravi
just made as well as Amna

01:25:03.770 --> 01:25:08.770
is that one gets rewards
in the economics profession

01:25:10.390 --> 01:25:13.840
by publishing in certain kinds of journals

01:25:13.840 --> 01:25:17.560
and you'd publish in
certain kind of journals by,

01:25:17.560 --> 01:25:21.473
on the basis of
methodological sophistication.

01:25:22.350 --> 01:25:25.960
So when you start asking why
aren't economists interfacing

01:25:25.960 --> 01:25:29.910
with economic history
and with anthropologists

01:25:29.910 --> 01:25:32.230
or political scientists more,

01:25:32.230 --> 01:25:35.220
that kind of is already your answer.

01:25:35.220 --> 01:25:40.220
You, you're gonna be posing
questions that are broader

01:25:40.490 --> 01:25:45.000
than the ones that can
just solely be answered

01:25:45.000 --> 01:25:48.210
with the kinds of methodological tools

01:25:48.210 --> 01:25:52.460
that are going to get you
published in these journals.

01:25:52.460 --> 01:25:55.770
So we're kind of interested in asking

01:25:55.770 --> 01:26:00.770
some of these broader questions
and asking how might we,

01:26:01.135 --> 01:26:02.730
what methodological means

01:26:02.730 --> 01:26:05.170
can we use to approach those questions?

01:26:05.170 --> 01:26:09.130
So I hope that the people
who were here with us today

01:26:09.130 --> 01:26:12.903
can continue with us to
get some of those answers.

01:26:14.317 --> 01:26:15.289
- Thank you Gerry.

01:26:15.289 --> 01:26:16.990
Rohini, we'll go to you.

01:26:16.990 --> 01:26:18.460
- Thanks, I don't think
I have much to add.

01:26:18.460 --> 01:26:20.110
The one last point I'd probably add

01:26:20.110 --> 01:26:22.360
and maybe this goes back
to the discussion of

01:26:23.329 --> 01:26:25.630
where did the inequality
discussion go in development

01:26:25.630 --> 01:26:28.230
is that we are also hoping to,

01:26:28.230 --> 01:26:30.840
in a lot of ways not silo development.

01:26:30.840 --> 01:26:34.946
I mean development was a very
old product as a discipline,

01:26:34.946 --> 01:26:36.121
it was a very old product.

01:26:36.121 --> 01:26:38.980
It was defined by, at
the end of colonialism

01:26:38.980 --> 01:26:42.850
and most fields in economics
are not defined by country.

01:26:42.850 --> 01:26:44.734
They are defined by for instance,

01:26:44.734 --> 01:26:45.736
if you're thinking about labor economics,

01:26:45.736 --> 01:26:47.910
you're thinking about
issues in public economics.

01:26:47.910 --> 01:26:51.380
And today, the field of
development in some ways

01:26:51.380 --> 01:26:55.603
is perhaps killed by its
own success in that it,

01:26:56.580 --> 01:26:58.400
if you're talking about issues of say,

01:26:58.400 --> 01:27:00.620
affirmative action and inequality,

01:27:00.620 --> 01:27:02.410
those conversations should be happening

01:27:02.410 --> 01:27:05.490
across people thinking
about them in the U.S.,

01:27:05.490 --> 01:27:07.490
in Malaysia, in India

01:27:07.490 --> 01:27:09.707
and yet sometimes that gets very siloed.

01:27:10.840 --> 01:27:12.460
Issues of inequality need to be dealt

01:27:12.460 --> 01:27:14.930
by thinking about tax
systems and public finance

01:27:14.930 --> 01:27:18.100
but that's, if you look at
the public finance literature,

01:27:18.100 --> 01:27:21.830
that's usually a very U.S.
focused literature in economics.

01:27:21.830 --> 01:27:25.330
So I think one thing that I
would love to see come out

01:27:25.330 --> 01:27:29.100
over the next few years as we
broaden these conversations

01:27:29.100 --> 01:27:31.940
is really to say that when
we ask these questions,

01:27:31.940 --> 01:27:33.287
we shouldn't really think about it,

01:27:33.287 --> 01:27:34.147
"Oh it's a development

01:27:34.147 --> 01:27:35.937
"because it's talking about Nigeria

01:27:35.937 --> 01:27:38.347
"and it's not development
because it's talking about

01:27:38.347 --> 01:27:39.970
"the US or UK."

01:27:39.970 --> 01:27:41.720
But rather think about
what the question is

01:27:41.720 --> 01:27:44.520
and I hope that this conference
gets us started on that.

01:27:45.480 --> 01:27:47.170
- Thanks Rohini, great points.

01:27:47.170 --> 01:27:50.070
So that concludes this opening
session of the symposium

01:27:50.070 --> 01:27:51.470
and again, this is just the beginning

01:27:51.470 --> 01:27:53.740
of a virtual symposium devoted to race,

01:27:53.740 --> 01:27:56.800
ethnicity, gender and economic
justice around the world.

01:27:56.800 --> 01:27:58.750
Hosted by Yale's Economic Growth Center

01:27:58.750 --> 01:28:00.500
and Inclusion Economics Yale.

01:28:00.500 --> 01:28:02.640
It will continue on Friday October 29th

01:28:02.640 --> 01:28:05.510
and Friday November 5th
with researchers presenting

01:28:05.510 --> 01:28:07.830
on the costs of
discrimination based on race,

01:28:07.830 --> 01:28:10.870
ethnicity and gender and
paths to economic justice.

01:28:10.870 --> 01:28:13.340
You can see information
on registration here

01:28:13.340 --> 01:28:15.450
in Zoom if you're joining us live.

01:28:15.450 --> 01:28:17.340
We really appreciate all
of you in the audience

01:28:17.340 --> 01:28:19.070
for taking the time to tune in.

01:28:19.070 --> 01:28:21.330
Thanks for your great questions.

01:28:21.330 --> 01:28:22.990
Look forward to continuing
the conversation

01:28:22.990 --> 01:28:25.863
during the symposium and
thanks again to our panelists.

